<td id="S2.T1.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">First <math id="S2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tokens of the text</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Examples of inputs and context variables.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Labels.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the label of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Labels can take different forms, such as discrete categories in classification, continuous values in regression, or structured outputs like text captions of images in vision-language models.
Labels may be obtained from human annotators, or in pseudo-forms such as clusters.
Typically, labels are provided as compatible pairs of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,a)</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from the joint distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Random transformations.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These are perturbations (augmentations) on the inputs that presumably do not change the semantics of the inputs by too much.
In this case, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the corrupted version of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, transformations for images include translation, rotation, flipping, masking, Gaussian noise, Cutout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and Mixup <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib169" title="" class="ltx_ref">169</a>]</cite>.
<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is provided by the transformation such that one can sample <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A\sim P^{+}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sim P^{+}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math> for arbitrarily many times.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Graphs.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Graph data is very common in industry, such as social networks, drug discovery, cybersecurity, etc.
Graphs also appear in domains that do not involve with graphs explicitly.
For example, K-nearest neighbors (KNN) gives a graph where every sample is connected to its nearest neighbors.
Graphs can also be continuous: for example, manifold learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> approximates a differential operator such as the Laplace-Beltrami operator on a manifold with the continuous limit of a family of graphs.
For graphs, we have <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(a|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(a|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is proportional to the weight of the edge between <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Feature maps and teacher models.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both are functions that map <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a feature encoding <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically a Hilbert space.
Feature maps are usually defined by humans, while teacher models are learned from data.
For example, feature maps for images include PNG, JPEG, etc.
Feature maps can also be implicitly defined by kernels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib124" title="" class="ltx_ref">124</a>]</cite>.
A popular example of teacher models is pretrained language models released by tech companies.
It is important to note that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is possible that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown.
For example, for these LLMs, if they are close-sourced, then we do not know how they are exactly pretrained.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Spectral Properties of a Context</h3>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The joint distribution <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> induces an operator from <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we call the expectation operator. Basically, it computes the conditional expectation of a function <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This operator is very intuitive: Suppose we want to predict for an input <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, but we only have a predictor <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on space <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>; the most reasonable prediction in this scenario is <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[g(A)|x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[g(A)|x]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The adjoint operator of the expectation operator is also an expectation operator, but in the reverse direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.1</span></span><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">expectation operator</span> <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left(T_{P^{+}}g\right)}(x)=\int g(a)P^{+}(a|x)da=\mathbb{E}[g(A)|x]\qquad\text{for all }g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left(T_{P^{+}}g\right)}(x)=\int g(a)P^{+}(a|x)da=\mathbb{E}[g(A)|x]\qquad\text{for all }g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Its <span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">adjoint operator</span> <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, which satisfies <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f,T_{P^{+}}g\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=\langle T_{P^{+}}^{*}f,g\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f,T_{P^{+}}g\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=\langle T_{P^{+}}^{*}f,g\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left(T_{P^{+}}^{*}f\right)}(a)=\int f(x)\frac{P^{+}(a|x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x)}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}dx=\mathbb{E}[f(X)|a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left(T_{P^{+}}^{*}f\right)}(a)=\int f(x)\frac{P^{+}(a|x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x)}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}dx=\mathbb{E}[f(X)|a]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.2</span></span><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In general, the operator <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the data distribution <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
because the stochastic mapping <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\mapsto A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\mapsto A</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on the distribution of <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, the mapping from an image to its label is independent of the data distribution on the image space.
On the other hand, the adjoint operator <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> due to Bayes’ rule.
For example, given the same label “dog”, a class-conditional generative model trained on CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite> and another one trained on ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">120</a>]</cite> will generate very different images.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An easier way to understand these operators is to use the following shorthand notations.
Since computing <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> requires drawing <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{+}(\cdot|X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(\cdot|X)</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}:X\rightarrow A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}:X\rightarrow A</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, we can write <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}:A\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}:A\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can also compose the two operators as <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}:X\rightarrow A\rightarrow X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}:X\rightarrow A\rightarrow X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that we first sample <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A\sim P^{+}(\cdot|X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sim P^{+}(\cdot|X)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then sample <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X^{\prime}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|A)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, we have <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}:A\rightarrow X\rightarrow A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}:A\rightarrow X\rightarrow A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> are self-adjoint operators, and they are the integral operators of the following <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p.s.d.</span> kernels.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.3</span></span><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positive-pair kernel</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite> <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{A}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{A}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> and its <span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dual kernel</span> <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{P^{+}(a,a^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})}=\frac{\int P^{+}(a|x)P^{+}(a^{\prime}|x)d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x)}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{P^{+}(a,a^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})}=\frac{\int P^{+}(a|x)P^{+}(a^{\prime}|x)d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x)}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime})};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{P^{+}(x,x^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})}=\frac{\int P^{+}(x|a)P^{+}(x^{\prime}|a)d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})}=\int\frac{P^{+}(a|x)P^{+}(a|x^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}da." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{P^{+}(x,x^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})}=\frac{\int P^{+}(x|a)P^{+}(x^{\prime}|a)d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})}=\int\frac{P^{+}(a|x)P^{+}(a|x^{\prime})}{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}da.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Their integral operators are given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\qquad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}\qquad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\left(T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}g\right)}(a)=\int g(a^{\prime}){k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime});" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\left(T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}g\right)}(a)=\int g(a^{\prime}){k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a^{\prime});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\qquad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}=T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\qquad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\left(T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}f\right)}(x)=\int f(x^{\prime}){k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\left(T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}f\right)}(x)=\int f(x^{\prime}){k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}:f\mapsto\int f(x^{\prime})k(\cdot,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}:f\mapsto\int f(x^{\prime})k(\cdot,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the integral operator of any kernel <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Throughout this work, we assume that <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are both Hilbert-Schmidt operators.
We say that <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">eigenvalue</span> of <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">eigenfunction</span> <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}\nu=\lambda\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}\nu=\lambda\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under the above assumption, by Hilbert-Schmidt theorem, we can order the eigenvalues by <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\lambda_{0}\geq\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\lambda_{0}\geq\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding eigenfunctions <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0},\nu_{1},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0},\nu_{1},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> form an orthonormal basis (ONB) of <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here <math id="S3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> because of Jensen’s inequality,
and it is easy to see that <math id="S3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> is always an eigenfunction of <math id="S3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, denote the eigenfunctions and eigenvalues of <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we can order the eigenvalues by <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\kappa_{0}\geq\kappa_{1}\geq\cdots\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\kappa_{0}\geq\kappa_{1}\geq\cdots\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0},\mu_{1},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0},\mu_{1},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> form an ONB of <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The two sets of eigenfunctions have the following connection.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.4</span></span><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Duality property)<span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}=\kappa_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}=\kappa_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. And if <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
For any <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}=\lambda_{i}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}=\lambda_{i}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}=\lambda_{i}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}=\lambda_{i}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which shows that <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an eigenfunction of <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with eigenvalue <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\left\langle\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i},\lambda_{j}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\left\langle\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i},\lambda_{j}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\lambda_{j}^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\left\langle T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i},T_{P^{+}}\nu_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\lambda_{j}^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\left\langle T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i},\nu_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\lambda_{j}^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\left\langle T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i},T_{P^{+}}\nu_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\lambda_{j}^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\left\langle T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i},\nu_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\lambda_{j}^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\left\langle\lambda_{i}\nu_{i},\nu_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}={\mathbb{I}}[i=j]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\lambda_{j}^{-\frac{1}{2}}{\left\langle\lambda_{i}\nu_{i},\nu_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}={\mathbb{I}}[i=j],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which implies that <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\left\{\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}\right\}}_{i:\lambda_{i}&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\{\lambda_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i}\right\}}_{i:\lambda_{i}&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthonormal.
Similarly, all <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> are eigenvalues of <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\left\{\kappa_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}\mu_{i}\right\}}_{i:\kappa_{i}&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\{\kappa_{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}}T_{P^{+}}^{*}\mu_{i}\right\}}_{i:\kappa_{i}&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthonormal. This implies the result.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This result leads to the singular value decomposition (SVD) of <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=\lambda_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">singular value</span> of <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, associated with the left <span id="S3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">singular function</span> <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and the right singular function <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since we choose <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>. all other <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) must have zero mean because they are orthogonal to <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Moreover, we have the following spectral decomposition of <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.5</span></span><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Spectral decomposition)<span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(x,a)=\sum_{i}s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)\nu_{i}(a){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(x,a)=\sum_{i}s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)\nu_{i}(a){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
<math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i,\;\big\langle\frac{P^{+}(x,a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)},\nu_{i}\big\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\int P^{+}(a|x)\nu_{i}(a)da=(T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i})(x)=\big(\lambda_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mu_{i}\big)(x)=s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i,\;\big\langle\frac{P^{+}(x,a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)},\nu_{i}\big\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\int P^{+}(a|x)\nu_{i}(a)da=(T_{P^{+}}\nu_{i})(x)=\big(\lambda_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mu_{i}\big)(x)=s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\{\nu_{i}\right\}}_{i\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\{\nu_{i}\right\}}_{i\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an ONB, we have <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{P^{+}(x,a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)\nu_{i}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{P^{+}(x,a)}{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x){P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)}=\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}s_{i}\mu_{i}(x)\nu_{i}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This result immediately leads to the following spectral decomposition of the two kernels.
The proof is left as an exercise to the reader.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.6</span></span><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})=\sum s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x)\mu_{i}(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})=\sum s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x)\mu_{i}(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and <math id="chapter1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})=\sum s_{i}^{2}\nu_{i}(a)\nu_{i}(a^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{A}^{+}}(a,a^{\prime})=\sum s_{i}^{2}\nu_{i}(a)\nu_{i}(a^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
