#000000" stroke-width="1.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 334.65 0 L 411.71 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" color="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.28pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 411.71 0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -3.15 4.21 C -2.89 2.63 0 0.26 0.79 0 C 0 -0.26 -2.89 -2.63 -3.15 -4.21"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 337.73 14.96)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.24em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="72.57"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S6.F4.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">Reconstruct</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Two widely used self-supervised learning algorithms with random transformations. <span id="S6.F4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Multi-view learning. <span id="S6.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right:</span> Reconstruction.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us start with multi-view learning.
If <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,A^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,A^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> are two views independently drawn from <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{+}(\cdot|X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(\cdot|X)</annotation></semantics></math> for the same <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,A^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,A^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positive pair</span>.
If <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,A^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,A^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> are independently drawn from <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that they can be views of different inputs, then <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,A^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,A^{-})</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">negative pair</span>.
Multi-view learning trains an encoder <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> by enforcing <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(A)\approx\Psi(A^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(A)\approx\Psi(A^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> is an encoder on <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the required <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> should be an encoder on <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although it has been common practice to directly apply <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> at downstream whenever possible, such a practice is not theoretically correct.
For example, a BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> is trained on masked sentences and it never sees a complete sentence at pretrain time; as such, one cannot assume that the embeddings it gives to complete sentences retain 100% quality, even though it works well on many real tasks.
The theoretically correct way of using <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> is converting it to <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> by means of the <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">average encoder</span>:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi=T_{P^{+}}\Psi;\qquad\Phi(x)=\mathbb{E}_{A\sim P^{+}(\cdot|x)}{\left[\Psi(A)\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=T_{P^{+}}\Psi;\qquad\Phi(x)=\mathbb{E}_{A\sim P^{+}(\cdot|x)}{\left[\Psi(A)\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For any input <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated via Monte Carlo: first sample a number of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{+}(\cdot|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(\cdot|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then take the mean of <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This Monte Carlo estimation requires <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-access to the context, as defined in <a href="#chapter1.Thmtheorem10" title="Definition 1.10. ‣ 4 Three Types of Access and Example Contexts ‣ Chapter 1 Introduction to the Contexture Theory ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-view learning also has the feature collapse problem.
If only <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(A)\approx\Psi(A^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(A)\approx\Psi(A^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> is enforced, then one degenerate solution is to give all <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> the same embedding.
There are two popular solutions to this problem: contrastive learning and non-contrastive learning.
Contrastive learning brings the embeddings of negative pairs far apart, that is increasing the distance between <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(A)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(A^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(A^{-})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Non-contrastive learning has two types: asymmetry-based and constraint-based.
The asymmetry-based approach applies two encoders with slight (training or architectural) differences to the same <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, BYOL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> updates one encoder with exponential moving average (EMA) and the other in the standard way to create asymmetry, and SimSiam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> puts stop gradient on one of the encoders but not the other.
The constraint-based approach, such as Barlow Twins <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib163" title="" class="ltx_ref">163</a>]</cite> and VICReg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, uses the same orthonormality constraint as <a href="#S6.E4" title="In Graphs. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let us demonstrate that multi-view learning can learn the contexture with two example objectives.
The spectral contrastive loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> is given by</p>
<table id="S6.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}(\Psi)=\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A,A^{+}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|X)}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A^{-}\sim{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[-{\left\langle\tilde{\Psi}(A),\tilde{\Psi}(A^{+})\right\rangle}+\frac{1}{2}{\left\langle\tilde{\Psi}(A),\tilde{\Psi}(A^{-})\right\rangle}^{2}\right]};" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒜</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}(\Psi)=\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A,A^{+}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|X)}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A^{-}\sim{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[-{\left\langle\tilde{\Psi}(A),\tilde{\Psi}(A^{+})\right\rangle}+\frac{1}{2}{\left\langle\tilde{\Psi}(A),\tilde{\Psi}(A^{-})\right\rangle}^{2}\right]};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and constraint-based non-contrastive learning solves the following problem:</p>
<table id="S6.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\underset{\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A,A^{+}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|X)}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left\|\Psi(A)-\Psi(A^{+})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left[\Psi\right]}={\bm{I}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒜</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></munder><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A,A^{+}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|X)}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left\|\Psi(A)-\Psi(A^{+})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\qquad\text{s.t.}\qquad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left[\Psi\right]}={\bm{I}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.9</span></span><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter8.A5" title="Appendix 8.E Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 8 Proofs for Chapter 2 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.E</span></a>)<span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be any minimizer of <a href="#S6.E5" title="In Self-supervised learning (SSL) with random transformations. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_markedasmath"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, or any optimal solution to <a href="#S6.E6" title="In Self-supervised learning (SSL) with random transformations. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Then, <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Psi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Psi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}^{*}=T_{P^{+}}\tilde{\Psi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}^{*}=T_{P^{+}}\tilde{\Psi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> learns the contexture of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For reconstruction, suppose <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let the predictor be <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}\Psi(a)+{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}\Psi(a)+{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟎</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> if the predictor is unbiased.
The pretraining objective is given by</p>
<table id="S6.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}(\Psi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}\times d},\;{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}}}\;\underset{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\mathbb{E}}\;{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Psi(A)+{\bm{b}}-X\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}(\Psi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}\times d},\;{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{X}}}\;\underset{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\mathbb{E}}\;{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Psi(A)+{\bm{b}}-X\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.10</span></span><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be any minimizer of <a href="#S6.E7" title="In Self-supervised learning (SSL) with random transformations. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. Then, <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Psi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Psi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the integral operator of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{x},\tilde{x}^{\prime}\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{x},\tilde{x}^{\prime}\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> if the predictor is biased, or <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle x,x^{\prime}\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle x,x^{\prime}\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math> if the predictor is unbiased.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
The proof is the same as <a href="#chapter2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6 (Proof in ). ‣ Regression tasks. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, which is left as an exercise.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The three illustrate examples demonstrate that many existing popular variational objectives can already learn the contexture.
The next step is to make them more general so that they can be adapted to a wider variety of contexts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>General Objectives: SVME and KISE</h3>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4" title="4 Three Types of Access and Example Contexts ‣ Chapter 1 Introduction to the Contexture Theory ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> introduced three types of access to a context: pair access, kernel access (<math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access), and transformation access (<math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-access).
This section presents two general objectives: SVME for pair access, and KISE for <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access.
SVME can also be used for <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-access since it is stronger than pair access.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To motivate SVME, think about the weaknesses of contrastive and non-contrastive learning objectives in <a href="#S6.E5" title="In Self-supervised learning (SSL) with random transformations. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equations</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S6.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Self-supervised learning (SSL) with random transformations. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
First, they require the stronger <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-access, since for pair access, it is not always possible to draw two views of the same <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Second, using the average encoder is not ideal since it slows down inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single-view multi-encoder (SVME)</span> learning addresses both weaknesses.
It produces the encoder <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> directly, and needs only one view for each <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, it only requires pair access.
It does so at the cost of training more than one encoders.
SVME with two encoders is formulated as the following optimization problem.</p>
<table id="S7.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{SVME:}\quad\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d};\;\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\;\underset{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left\|\Phi(X)-\Psi(A)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\quad\textrm{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left[\Phi\right]}={\bm{I}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">SVME:</mtext><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒜</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{SVME:}\quad\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d};\;\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\;\underset{(X,A)\sim P^{+}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left\|\Phi(X)-\Psi(A)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\quad\textrm{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left[\Phi\right]}={\bm{I}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">SVME can be viewed as a combination of asymmetry-based and constraint-based non-contrastive learning.
It uses a similar double-encoder architecture as asymmetry-based non-contrastive learning, and the asymmetry here is that the constraint is only imposed on <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> but not <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Meanwhile, it uses the same orthonormality constraint as mentioned earlier.
If <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then similar to BYOL and SimSiam, one can implement <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> as two heads on top of the same neural network backbone.
SVME can have more than two encoders, which we shall see later in this chapter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">SVME is inspired by multi-modal learning.
For example, in vision-language models like CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">116</a>]</cite>, <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> can be the image encoder, and <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> can be the text encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access, usually <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is an approximation of the dual kernel.
For simplicity, let us assume that we have access to <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can estimate <math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}f</annotation></semantics></math> with Monte Carlo given a set of inputs.
The objective of <span id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">kernel-integral single-encoder (KISE)</span> learning is formulated as</p>
<table id="S7.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{KISE:}\quad\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\text{minimize}}\;\;\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;{\left[{\left\|\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}-{\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}(X),T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\rangle}\right]}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">KISE:</mtext><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{KISE:}\quad\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\text{minimize}}\;\;\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;{\left[{\left\|\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}-{\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}(X),T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\rangle}\right]}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Different from SVME, KISE only trains one encoder <math id="S7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.
KISE is similar to the spectral inference network (SpIN) method proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">113</a>]</cite>.
SpIN maximizes <math id="S7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}(X),T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}(X),T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>, while KISE is more numerically stable when using gradient methods because the objective is lower bounded by zero, provided that all eigenvalues of <math id="S7.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <math id="S7.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Maximizing SpIN might cause the model weights to explode, but minimizing KISE will not.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The following result shows that both SVME and KISE can learn the contexture of <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, and they are in fact equivalent.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.11</span></span><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter8.A6" title="Appendix 8.F Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 8 Proofs for Chapter 2 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.F</span></a>)<span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In <a href="#S7.E8" title="In 7 General Objectives: SVME and KISE ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, if <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> is substituted with the optimal <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, then this problem becomes equivalent to <a href="#S7.E9" title="In 7 General Objectives: SVME and KISE ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
Let <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be any optimal solution to <a href="#S7.E9" title="In 7 General Objectives: SVME and KISE ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, then <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> learns the contexture of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Converting <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access to <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-access.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is an additional way of using <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access, which requires the kernel <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to be always non-negative.
The idea is similar to the objective for graphs that we saw earlier.
Define the degree <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)=\int k(x,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)=\int k(x,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and define a new context <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(a|x)=k(x,a){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(a)/D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(a|x)=k(x,a){P_{\mathcal{X}}}(a)/D(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One has <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-access to this context if one can access <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual kernel of the original context, then we have <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any eigenfunction <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with eigenvalue <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}\mu_{i}=s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}\mu_{i}=s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since obviously <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}=T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}=T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a singular function of <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> with singular value <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, the context of <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> has the same singular functions as the original context, with all singular values squared.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementing the orthonormality constraint.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The orthonormality constraint in SVME and KISE can be implemented by VICReg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
Let <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\{(x_{1},a_{1}),\cdots,(x_{m},a_{m})\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\{(x_{1},a_{1}),\cdots,(x_{m},a_{m})\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a batch of training samples.
The VICReg objective is the sum of an invariance loss, a variance loss and the covariance loss, defined as</p>
<table id="S7.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(\Phi,\Psi)=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{k=1}^{m}{\left\|\Phi(x_{k})-\Psi(a_{k})\right\|}_{2}^{2}+\frac{\alpha}{d}\sum_{i=1}^{d}{\left(1-\sqrt{{\bm{C}}[i,i]+\epsilon}\right)}_{+}+\frac{\beta}{d(d-1)}\sum_{i\neq j}{\bm{C}}[i,j]^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(\Phi,\Psi)=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{k=1}^{m}{\left\|\Phi(x_{k})-\Psi(a_{k})\right\|}_{2}^{2}+\frac{\alpha}{d}\sum_{i=1}^{d}{\left(1-\sqrt{{\bm{C}}[i,i]+\epsilon}\right)}_{+}+\frac{\beta}{d(d-1)}\sum_{i\neq j}{\bm{C}}[i,j]^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)_{+}=\max{\left\{0,x\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)_{+}=\max{\left\{0,x\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ReLU function, <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small positive constant for numerical stability, and <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empirical covariance matrix: <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}[i,j]=\frac{1}{m-1}\sum_{k=1}^{m}\tilde{\phi}_{i}(x_{k})\tilde{\phi}_{j}(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}[i,j]=\frac{1}{m-1}\sum_{k=1}^{m}\tilde{\phi}_{i}(x_{k})\tilde{\phi}_{j}(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are two positive hyperparameters.
The first term is the invariance loss that aligns the two encoders.
The second term is the variance loss that pulls <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\tilde{\phi}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\tilde{\phi}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> closer to <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The third term is the covariance loss that makes the <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions orthogonal to each other.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although VICReg is used a lot in our experiments, we observe that the two regularization terms cannot enforce the orthonormality constraint perfectly.
In fact, both the variance loss and the covariance loss cannot converge to zero.
Alternatively, we tried projecting <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> to become orthonormal after each epoch, but doing so does not improve the performance of the encoder.
Finding better ways than VICReg to enforce the orthonormality constraint is posed as an open problem.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Distilling Knowledge from Teacher Models</h3>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This part discusses how to learn from the context given by a teacher model.
Not only does this have lots of applications in practice, but also it gives us a way to convert a context with any access to one with <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access, which will be very useful in later chapters.
Teacher models are very common in practice nowadays. Many big tech companies release their large language models or generative models, all of which can be viewed as teacher models.
However, using these models usually incurs a cost, and these models are often too large to be deployed locally (especially in academic labs).
As a result, knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> becomes very attractive—people would like to distill their knowledge to smaller and more affordable models of their own.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a teacher model, which is presumably learned from some context variable <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. If the model is close-sourced, then we cannot see <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> at all, and we may not even know what space <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is.
Even if the model is open-sourced and we know the space <math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, very few companies release <math id="S8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> as a part of their pretraining data.
Indeed, it appears that the quality of <math id="S8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> largely depends on the quality of <math id="S8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> (the quality of the context), so most companies keep it as their business secret.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Even though we might not know <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, it is still possible to distill the knowledge of <math id="S8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> if we can query <math id="S8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for a sufficient number of times.
We can construct its centered linear kernel <math id="S8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}_{t}(x),\tilde{\Phi}_{t}(x^{\prime})\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}(x,x^{\prime})={\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}_{t}(x),\tilde{\Phi}_{t}(x^{\prime})\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>, and use KISE to extract its top eigenspace.
It is easy to see that <math id="S8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> is an eigenfunction of <math id="S8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with eigenvalue <math id="S8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Two remarks on this method.
First, we still cannot access <math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> if the data is not released.
If we use our own data from another distribution during distillation, then the contexture will be skewed.
Second, the linear kernel <math id="S8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the teacher model is not equal to <math id="S8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, it contains the information of at most the top-<math id="S8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S8.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which nonetheless is already sufficient for knowledge distillation purpose.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to KISE, we can use the following objective for distillation:</p>
<table id="S8.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}(\Phi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{t}\times d},\;{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{t}}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Phi(X)+{\bm{b}}-\Phi_{t}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}(\Phi)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{t}\times d},\;{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{t}}}\;\mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Phi(X)+{\bm{b}}-\Phi_{t}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p5.2" class="ltx_p">This objective extracts the top-<math id="S8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="S8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with a (centered) linear kernel as the loss kernel. This can be proved in the same way as <a href="#chapter2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6 (Proof in ). ‣ Regression tasks. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.
We leave this proof as an exercise.
Let <math id="S8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dual kernel of the original context <math id="S8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> was trained on.
If <math id="S8.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="S8.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="S8.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and preserves their order as well, and <math id="S8.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq d_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq d_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, then apparently the <math id="S8.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> we learn will extract the top-<math id="S8.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="S8.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">However, one issue is that <math id="S8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> might not preserve the original order of the eigenfunctions.
Among all the objectives we have discussed so far, only the spectral contrastive loss <a href="#S6.E5" title="In Self-supervised learning (SSL) with random transformations. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> can preserve the original eigenvalues; all the other objectives can only recover the space spanned by the top eigenfunctions.
Therefore, given a teacher model, it is unsafe to assume that it preserves the original order of the eigenfunctions.
One way to completely erase the eigenfunctions of the teacher model is to whiten <math id="S8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> before constructing <math id="S8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is multiplying <math id="S8.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S8.p6.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\mathrm{Cov}(\Phi_{t})]^{-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Cov</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathrm{Cov}(\Phi_{t})]^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting <math id="S8.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> will have the same eigenfunctions as the original <math id="S8.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, but its eigenvalues become all either <math id="S8.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S8.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is called a spectrally transformed kernel (STK), which will be discussed in more detail in <a href="#chapter5" title="Chapter 5 Statistical Learning Bounds for Representation Learning ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Chapter</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
Note that whitening is not useful for knowledge distillation from a single teacher model, but will be quite useful later when there are multiple contexts.</p>
</div>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Converting any access to <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any pretrained encoder can be a teacher model.
Thus, for any context, we can first pretrain an encoder with it, view the encoder as a teacher model, and then obtain a context with <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access using the above approach.
This also makes storing contexts very simple.
Suppose a context is only available for a limited amount of time, for example due to copyright limitations.
All we need to do is to pretrain an encoder using the context and store it.
Then, we can use the context whenever we want later on.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Social impact.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whether knowledge distillation constitutes a copyright infringement is an important problem.
Recently, DeepSeek <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> showed that it is possible to use a fairly small budget to distill the knowledge of an OpenAI model that cost billions of dollars to pretrain.
Moreover, the analysis above shows that such knowledge distillation cannot be prevented by making the model close-sourced.
As such, big tech companies might be less and less incentivized to grant public access to their models at a low price, fearing that it would be too easy for other companies to copy their work.
This might slow down the development of AI.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Learning from a Mixture of Contexts</h3>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objectives discussed so far can only learn from one context.
In practice, it is often the case that there are multiple training signals that we can leverage.
For example, there might be multiple random transformations, such as translation, flipping, cropping and color distortion for images.
There could also be multiple labels for each sample, or labels and a graph.
Here we demonstrate that one can learn representations from multiple training signals by mixing multiple contexts,
and in fact some existing learning algorithms are implicitly doing this.
Specifically, we discuss two base operations: convolution and convex combination.
<a href="#chapter4" title="Chapter 4 Mixing Multiple Contexts ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Chapter</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> will provide a more general framework for mixing multiple contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose there are <math id="S9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> contexts given by <math id="S9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}_{1},\cdots,P^{+}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}_{1},\cdots,P^{+}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{P_{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{P_{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the marginal distribution of every <math id="S9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S9.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}_{1},\cdots,{k_{X}^{+}}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}_{1},\cdots,{k_{X}^{+}}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be their dual kernels.
Then, the <span id="S9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">convolution</span> of these contexts is defined to have the top eigenfunctions of <math id="S9.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{1}}T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{2}}\cdots T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{r}}\cdots T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{2}}T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{1}}T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{2}}\cdots T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{r}}\cdots T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{2}}T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> as its contexture;
and the <span id="S9.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">convex combination</span> of these contexts is defined to have the top eigenfunctions of <math id="S9.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}T_{{k_{X}^{+}}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> as its contexture, for some fixed non-negative <math id="S9.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The convolution usually appears when one composes multiple random transformations; for example, when one applies translation, flipping and cropping to the same image.
The convex combination appears when the objective is the weighted sum of multiple individual objectives, each of which is designed for one context, as illustrated in <a href="#S9.F5" title="In 9 Learning from a Mixture of Contexts ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S9.F5" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S9.F5.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="140.19" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 587.91 140.19" width="587.91"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,140.19) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(263.16,0) translate(0,11.04)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -39.07 -9.42 h 78.14 v 18.83 h -78.14 Z"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -34.46 -4.8)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="70.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S9.F5.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">Translation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 88.23 -10.76 h 59.77 v 21.52 h -59.77 Z"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 92.84 -3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="50.54"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S9.F5.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">Flipping</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 203.53 -10.68 h 65.38 v 21.37 h -65.38 Z"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 208.14 -3.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="56.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S9.F5.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">Cropping</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 131.72 59.06 C 131.72 66.57 125.63 72.66 118.11 72.66 C 110.6 72.66 104.5 66.57 104.5 59.06 C 104.5 51.54 110.6 45.45 118.11 45.45 C 125.63 45.45 131.72 51.54 131.72 59.06 Z M 118.11 59.06"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 112.73 55.6)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.58em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.08em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.22" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.07)" width="10.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.F5.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -258.54 -4.8)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="124.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S9.F5.pic1.4" class="ltx_text">Convex combination</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="1.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 19.38 9.69 L 104.5 52.25"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.96pt" transform="matrix(0.89445 0.44717 -0.44717 0.89445 104.5 52.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.66 3.54 C -2.44 2.21 0 0.22 0.66 0 C 0 -0.22 -2.44 -2.21 -2.66 -3.54"></path></g><g stroke-width="1.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 118.11 11.04 L 118.11 43.84"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.96pt" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 118.11 43.84)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.66 3.54 C -2.44 2.21 0 0.22 0.66 0 C 0 -0.22 -2.44 -2.21 -2.66 -3.54"></path></g><g stroke-width="1.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 214.31 10.96 L 131.72 52.25"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.96pt" transform="matrix(-0.89445 0.44716 -0.44716 -0.89445 131.72 52.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.66 3.54 C -2.44 2.21 0 0.22 0.66 0 C 0 -0.22 -2.44 -2.21 -2.66 -3.54"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -85.01 113.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="12.55"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.F5.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -39.07 108.69 h 78.14 v 18.83 h -78.14 Z"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -34.46 113.31)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="70.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S9.F5.pic1.5" class="ltx_text">Translation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 88.23 107.35 h 59.77 v 21.52 h -59.77 Z"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 92.84 114.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="50.54"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S9.F5.pic1.6" class="ltx_text">Flipping</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 203.53 107.43 h 65.38 v 21.37 h -65.38 Z"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 208.14 114.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.15" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="56.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S9.F5.pic1.7" class="ltx_text">Cropping</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 309.77 113.38)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S9.F5.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="1.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -67.58 118.11 L -40.68 118.11"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.96pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -40.68 118.11)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.66 3.54 C -2.44 2.21 0 0.22 0.66 0 C 0 -0.22 -2.44 -2.21 -2.66 -3.54"></path></g><g stroke-width="1.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 39.35 118.11 L 86.62 118.11"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.96pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 86.62 118.11)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.66 3.54 C -2.44 2.21 0 0.22 0.66 0 C 0 -0.22 -2.44 -2.21 -2.66 -3.54"></path></g><g stroke-width="1.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 148.27 118.11 L 201.93 118.11"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.96pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 201.93 118.11)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.66 3.54 C -2.44 2.21 0 0.22 0.66 0 C 0 -0.22 -2.44 -2.21 -2.66 -3.54"></path></g><g stroke-width="1.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 269.19 118.11 L 303.55 118.11"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.96pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 303.55 118.11)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.66 3.54 C -2.44 2.21 0 0.22 0.66 0 C 0 -0.22 -2.44 -2.21 -2.66 -3.54"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -233.56 113.31)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="74.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S9.F5.pic1.8" class="ltx_text">Convolution</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Convolution and convex combination of multiple transformations on images.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us look at three concrete examples: supervised learning with a feature map, multi-step generative models, and RLHF.</p>
</div>
<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Supervised learning with a feature map.</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a space of real-world objects, the inputs need to be mapped to numerical vectors via a feature map <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> so that they can be stored in a computer.
For example, if <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of images, then <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> can be PNG or JPEG.
The quality of <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> affects the quality of the context.
Generally speaking, PNG is better than JPEG because it is lossless, while JPEG loses information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a deterministic mapping. Define operator <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Omega}:{L^{2}(P_{\omega})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Omega}:{L^{2}(P_{\omega})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> and its adjoint operator <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Omega}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\omega})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Omega}^{*}:{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\rightarrow{L^{2}(P_{\omega})}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S9.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{\Omega}h)(x)=h(\Omega(x));\qquad(T_{\Omega}^{*}f)(\omega)=\int f(x)dP(x|\omega)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{\Omega}h)(x)=h(\Omega(x));\qquad(T_{\Omega}^{*}f)(\omega)=\int f(x)dP(x|\omega).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this scenario, an encoder <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> can be trained as follows.
First, train <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\omega}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\omega}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> via supervised learning.
Then, define <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=T_{\Omega}\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=T_{\Omega}\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
For classification tasks, similar to <a href="#S6" title="6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>,
<math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is trained using an unbiased predictor and the mean squared error:</p>
<table id="S9.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}(\Gamma)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{A}}}\times d},{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{A}}}}}\;\underset{(X,A)\sim P^{+},\;\omega=\Omega(X)}{\mathbb{E}}\;{\left[{\left\|A-{\bm{W}}\Gamma(\omega)-b\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">ω</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}(\Gamma)=\min_{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{A}}}\times d},{\bm{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{A}}}}}\;\underset{(X,A)\sim P^{+},\;\omega=\Omega(X)}{\mathbb{E}}\;{\left[{\left\|A-{\bm{W}}\Gamma(\omega)-b\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.12</span></span><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter8.A8" title="Appendix 8.H Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 8 Proofs for Chapter 2 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.H</span></a>)<span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> minimizes <a href="#S9.E11" title="In Supervised learning with a feature map. ‣ 9 Learning from a Mixture of Contexts ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> if and only if <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\Omega}^{*}T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\Omega}^{*}T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> (including <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>), where <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the integral operator of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\mathbb{I}}[a=a^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Lambda}(a,a^{\prime})={\mathbb{I}}[a=a^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}=T_{\Omega}\Gamma^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}=T_{\Omega}\Gamma^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k_{\Omega}}T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{k_{\Omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k_{\Omega}}T_{P^{+}}\Lambda T_{P^{+}}^{*}T_{k_{\Omega}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual kernel associated with <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k_{\Omega}}=T_{\Omega}T_{\Omega}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k_{\Omega}}=T_{\Omega}T_{\Omega}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to <a href="#chapter2.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 2.4 (Proof in ). ‣ Classification tasks. ‣ 6 Three Illustrative Examples ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, one can get rid of <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> using the balanced loss.
We can see that this <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> learns the convolution of two contexts, one given by <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> and the other given by <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One important application of supervised learning with a feature map is node representation learning on graphs.
Two contexts are available in this application: the graph, and each node has a node feature.
Popular methods such as graph neural networks (GNNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> train an encoder <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the node feature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-step generative models.</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are two extremely popular generative models nowadays—large language models and denoising diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib132" title="" class="ltx_ref">132</a>]</cite>.
Both models can generate input <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> from a starting point <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in a number of steps.
The starting point <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> may or may not contain information about <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
In large language models, <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a prompt, which contains partial information about <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and generation is done token by token;
in diffusion models, <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is white noise that has zero information about <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and generation is done by a number of denoising steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although generative models have achieved remarkable success in many applications, what representations these models are learning is quite unclear, and whether these representations can be applied to tasks other than generation is an active research topic.
For example, recently <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib159" title="" class="ltx_ref">159</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> studied whether the representations of diffusion models can be used for recognition tasks, and they found that these models “have strong recognition power for understanding the visual content”.
The common perception is that generative modeling and representation learning are two completely different paradigms in machine learning, but in fact they can be connected via the contexture theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A multi-step generative model, such as a diffusion model or a GPT, generates an input <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\rightarrow a_{2}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow a_{r}\rightarrow x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\rightarrow a_{2}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow a_{r}\rightarrow x</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains more information about <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> than <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The starting point <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> may or may not contain information about <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
For diffusion models, <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> plus Gaussian noise; for language models, <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> plus one more token at the end.
The following is a general formulation of multi-step generative modeling, which does not need to assume the specific form of <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be the joint distribution of <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use SVME to train <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+1)</annotation></semantics></math> encoders.
Specifically, we train an encoder <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}:{\mathcal{A}}_{j}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}:{\mathcal{A}}_{j}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, along with <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained with the goal of generating <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in one shot: Given <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}\in{\mathcal{A}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}\in{\mathcal{A}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we find the <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\hat{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\hat{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is the closest to <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}(a_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}(a_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, and this <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math> should be close to the original <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
The training objective for this goal is given by</p>
<table id="S9.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{j}(\Phi;\Psi_{j})=\mathbb{E}_{(X,A_{j})\sim P^{+}_{j}}\;{\left[{\left\|\Psi_{j}(A_{j})-\Phi(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{j}(\Phi;\Psi_{j})=\mathbb{E}_{(X,A_{j})\sim P^{+}_{j}}\;{\left[{\left\|\Psi_{j}(A_{j})-\Phi(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}=[w_{1},\cdots,w_{L}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}=[w_{1},\cdots,w_{L}]</annotation></semantics></math> be a weight vector where <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The overall objective is</p>
<table id="S9.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{\Phi,\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\;{\mathcal{R}}_{\textnormal{GEN}}(\Phi;\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r})=\sum_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}(\Phi;\Psi_{j})\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>GEN</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\Phi,\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\;{\mathcal{R}}_{\textnormal{GEN}}(\Phi;\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r})=\sum_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}(\Phi;\Psi_{j})\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Although each <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is pretrained with the goal of generating <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in one shot, actual generation is still performed in multiple steps.
Specifically, given <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}\in{\mathcal{A}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}\in{\mathcal{A}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, we find <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j+1}\in{\mathcal{A}}_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j+1}\in{\mathcal{A}}_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j+1}(a_{j+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j+1}(a_{j+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the closest to <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}(a_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}(a_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Such a procedure resembles a denoising diffusion model, which trains a noise approximator <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(\cdot,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(\cdot,j)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[r]</annotation></semantics></math> by closing the gap between <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}-\epsilon_{\theta}(a_{j},j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}-\epsilon_{\theta}(a_{j},j)</annotation></semantics></math> (this objective aims to denoise <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in one shot),
while the actual generation is done in multiple steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">One great thing about SVME is that even though there are lots of encoders, the constraint is only imposed on <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following result shows that this objective learns the contexture of a convex combination of the <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> contexts.
It is a corollary of the general result in <a href="#S16" title="16 Convex Combination ‣ Chapter 4 Mixing Multiple Contexts ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>, so the proof will be deferred until then.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.13</span></span><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dual kernel of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be any optimal solution to <a href="#S9.E12" title="In Multi-step generative models. ‣ 9 Learning from a Mixture of Contexts ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.
Then, <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}w_{j}{k_{X}^{+}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}w_{j}{k_{X}^{+}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It should be clarified that both diffusion models and large language models use a single-model architecture, commonly known as a decoder-only architecture, but can also be called an encoder-only architecture if the model is used as a representation.
The above analysis does not apply to encoder-decoder architectures such as VAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite> and GAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, because the representations of these encoders are meaningless without the corresponding decoders.
The output of VAE and GAN is a Gaussian random vector, whose association with <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is indecipherable without the decoder.
Therefore, VAE and GAN cannot be analyzed in the same way as above.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Alignment in RLHF.</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning with human feedback (RLHF) is a common technique in fine-tuning LLMs.
Let <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a reference model, such as a model trained by supervised fine-tuning (SFT).
Let <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be two random variables on <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> with possibly different distributions, both of which are associated with <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
For instance, <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> can be an English sentence, and <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be two Chinese translations of the same sentence.
There is also a critic function <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow{\left\{1,2\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow{\left\{1,2\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for any input <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred; otherwise, <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred.
The goal of RLHF is to fine-tune the model to learn such preference (which is also known as alignment), while still keeping the model close to <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In practice, it is observed that if <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is too far away from <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then its performance will be very poor, a phenomenon known as over-optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.
Thus, we need to make sure that <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> does not go too far away from <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\textrm{align}}(\Phi,\Psi)=\sum_{i=1,2}\;\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\Pr}{\left[C(X)=i\right]}\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left\|\Phi(X)-\Psi(A_{i})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\;\middle|\;C(X)=i\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\textrm{align}}(\Phi,\Psi)=\sum_{i=1,2}\;\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\Pr}{\left[C(X)=i\right]}\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left\|\Phi(X)-\Psi(A_{i})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\;\middle|\;C(X)=i\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> be the alignment loss based on SVME.
To implement this loss, for each sample <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,a_{1},a_{2},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,a_{1},a_{2},c)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the critic output, one simply updates the model to minimize <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\Phi(x)-\Psi(a_{1})\right\|}_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\Phi(x)-\Psi(a_{1})\right\|}_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\Phi(x)-\Psi(a_{2})\right\|}_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\Phi(x)-\Psi(a_{2})\right\|}_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, define a loss that reflects the gap between <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\textrm{ref}}(\Phi)=\underset{{\bm{W}},{\bm{b}}}{\min}\;\mathbb{E}{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Phi(X)+{\bm{b}}-\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝑾</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝒃</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\textrm{ref}}(\Phi)=\underset{{\bm{W}},{\bm{b}}}{\min}\;\mathbb{E}{\left[{\left\|{\bm{W}}\Phi(X)+{\bm{b}}-\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This loss function is invariant under invertible linear transformations on <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, because such transformations have no impact on the downstream performance when <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is used with a linear probe.
The overall objective is given by</p>
<table id="S9.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{\Phi,\Psi}{\textrm{minimize}}\quad{\mathcal{R}}_{\textrm{align}}(\Phi,\Psi)+\beta{\mathcal{R}}_{\textrm{ref}}(\Phi)\quad\text{s.t.}\qquad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></mrow></munder><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\Phi,\Psi}{\textrm{minimize}}\quad{\mathcal{R}}_{\textrm{align}}(\Phi,\Psi)+\beta{\mathcal{R}}_{\textrm{ref}}(\Phi)\quad\text{s.t.}\qquad\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for some <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similar to generative models, this objective also learns the contexture of a convex combination of two contexts.
The first context is given by a the random variable <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{+}(x,a)=\Pr[C(x)=1]P_{A_{1}}^{+}(x,a)+\Pr[C(x)=2]P_{A_{2}}^{+}(x,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(x,a)=\Pr[C(x)=1]P_{A_{1}}^{+}(x,a)+\Pr[C(x)=2]P_{A_{2}}^{+}(x,a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The second context is provided by the teacher model <math id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\textrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, similar to <a href="#S8.E10" title="In 8 Distilling Knowledge from Teacher Models ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues</h3>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">So far we have seen that a variety of variational objectives can extract the top-<math id="S10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="S10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that they can recover the linear space spanned by <math id="S10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One might ask if it is possible to extract the exact top-<math id="S10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions using a variational objective instead of kernel PCA.
This is indeed possible.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> proposed <span id="S10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">neural eigenfunctions</span> to extract the exact top-<math id="S10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It solves the following problem:</p>
<table id="S10.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S10.E13X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E13X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E13X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}{\left[{\left\|\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}-{\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}(X),T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\rangle}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}{\left[{\left\|\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}-{\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}(X),T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\rangle}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S10.E13Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E13Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{ s.t.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>s.t.</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{ s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.E13Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\displaystyle\left\|\tilde{\phi}_{i}\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}^{2}=1,\quad\forall i\neq j:\;{\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mo>⟨</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\displaystyle\left\|\tilde{\phi}_{i}\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}^{2}=1,\quad\forall i\neq j:\;{\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Its difference from KISE is that it changes the constraint <math id="S10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which enforces the different dimensions to be orthogonal, to <math id="S10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Interestingly, this small change allows the extraction of the exact eigenfunctions.
It should be emphasized that the <math id="S10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions are obtained simultaneously, not sequentially.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.14</span></span><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter8.A7" title="Appendix 8.G Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 8 Proofs for Chapter 2 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.G</span></a>)<span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the optimal <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S10.E13" title="In 10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> be <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\phi_{1}^{*},\cdots,\phi_{d}^{*}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{1}^{*},\cdots,\phi_{d}^{*}]</annotation></semantics></math>, then we can choose the eigenfunctions <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that have non-increasing eigenvalues and form an ONB of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\phi}_{1}^{*},\cdots,\tilde{\phi}_{d}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\phi}_{1}^{*},\cdots,\tilde{\phi}_{d}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a permutation of <math id="chapter2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With <math id="S10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-access, we can use multi-view learning to extract the exact eigenfunctions.</p>
<table id="S10.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\underset{\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\mathbb{E}{\left[{\left\|\tilde{\Psi}(A)-\tilde{\Psi}(A^{+})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\quad\text{ s.t. }\;{\left\|\tilde{\psi}_{i}\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{A}}}^{2}=1,\;\;\forall i\neq j:\;\mathbb{E}[\tilde{\psi}_{i}(A)\tilde{\psi}_{j}(A^{+})]=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒜</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mtext> s.t. </mtext><msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0.280em">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\Psi:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\mathbb{E}{\left[{\left\|\tilde{\Psi}(A)-\tilde{\Psi}(A^{+})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]}\quad\text{ s.t. }\;{\left\|\tilde{\psi}_{i}\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{A}}}^{2}=1,\;\;\forall i\neq j:\;\mathbb{E}[\tilde{\psi}_{i}(A)\tilde{\psi}_{j}(A^{+})]=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,A^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,A^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> are positive samples drawn from <math id="S10.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{+}(\cdot|X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(\cdot|X)</annotation></semantics></math> of the same <math id="S10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can prove that <math id="S10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the exact top-<math id="S10.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S10.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{A}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the same way as <a href="#chapter2.Thmtheorem14" title="Theorem 2.14 (Proof in ). ‣ 10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a>. And by <a href="#chapter1.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 1.4 (Duality property). ‣ 3 Spectral Properties of a Context ‣ Chapter 1 Introduction to the Contexture Theory ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, the average encoder <math id="S10.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}=T_{P^{+}}\tilde{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}=T_{P^{+}}\tilde{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> also extracts the exact top-<math id="S10.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S10.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> multiplied by some constants.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Post-hoc approach.</h4>

<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose we have a pretrained <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> that learns the contexture, can we obtain the exact eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>?
The answer is yes.
Since <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> does not necessarily contain the information of the exact eigenvalues and eigenfunctions, we still need to use the context, via either kernel access or pair access.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, suppose we have kernel access to the context.
Since <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> spans the same space as <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to learn a matrix <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}{\bm{Q}}=[\alpha_{1}\mu_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{d}\mu_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑸</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}{\bm{Q}}=[\alpha_{1}\mu_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{d}\mu_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{d}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{d}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, these <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be eliminated by normalizing <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}{\bm{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}{\bm{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> to have unit variance in each dimension.
The matrix <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the eigenvalues can be estimated as follows:</p>
<ol id="S10.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S10.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Estimate the covariance matrix <math id="S10.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]</annotation></semantics></math> with Monte Carlo.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Estimate <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S10.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}[i,j]={\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}[i,j]={\left\langle\tilde{\phi}_{i},T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}\tilde{\phi}_{j}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with Monte Carlo.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Solve the generalized eigenvalue problem <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}=\lambda{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}=\lambda{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the eigenvalues be <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{d}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{d}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the orthonormal eigenvectors be <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}_{1},\cdots,{\bm{v}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}_{1},\cdots,{\bm{v}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}}=[{\bm{v}}_{1},\cdots,{\bm{v}}_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}}=[{\bm{v}}_{1},\cdots,{\bm{v}}_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an estimation of the <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th eigenvalue of <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S10.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is not very large, this eigen-decomposition is efficient.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us elaborate on why this method works.
For simplicity, assume that the top-<math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues of <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct; without this assumption, the result can still be proved with a more verbose proof.
Let <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=[\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=[\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}=U{\bm{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}=U{\bm{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some invertible <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{R}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑹</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{R}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}[U]={\bm{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}[U]={\bm{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle U,T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}U\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}={\textnormal{diag}}{\left\{s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle U,T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}U\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}={\textnormal{diag}}{\left\{s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}={\bm{R}}^{\top}{\bm{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑹</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}={\bm{R}}^{\top}{\bm{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}={\bm{R}}^{\top}{\textnormal{diag}}{\left\{s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}\right\}}{\bm{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑹</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}={\bm{R}}^{\top}{\textnormal{diag}}{\left\{s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}\right\}}{\bm{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the generalized eigenvalues are equal to <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{R}}{\bm{v}}_{i}=\alpha_{i}{\bm{e}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{R}}{\bm{v}}_{i}=\alpha_{i}{\bm{e}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{e}}_{i}=[0,\cdots,0,1,0,\cdots,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{e}}_{i}=[0,\cdots,0,1,0,\cdots,0]</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{R}}{\bm{Q}}={\textnormal{diag}}{\left\{\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{d}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑸</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{R}}{\bm{Q}}={\textnormal{diag}}{\left\{\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{d}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}{\bm{Q}}=[\alpha_{1}\mu_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{d}\mu_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑸</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}{\bm{Q}}=[\alpha_{1}\mu_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{d}\mu_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Second, if we have pair access instead of <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access, then the eigenvalues and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained as follows:
Let <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> be trained via SVME.
We can estimate <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Psi}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}[\Psi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Psi}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}[\Psi]</annotation></semantics></math> via Monte Carlo.
Then similarly, we solve the generalized eigenvalue problem <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}=\lambda{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}=\lambda{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}{\bm{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the eigenvalues and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, if our goal is only to estimate the eigenvalues but not the eigenfunctions, then <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> need not to be trained with the entire dataset.
In fact, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib125" title="" class="ltx_ref">125</a>]</cite> showed that for any fixed <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, the sum <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated with low error using <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(d)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.i.d.</span> samples.
By union bound, all <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated with low error using <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(d\log d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(d\log d)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.i.d.</span> samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Estimating eigenvalues with the post-hoc approach.</h4>

<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us demonstrate the post-hoc method on 3 real datasets from OpenML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib146" title="" class="ltx_ref">146</a>]</cite>: <span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span>, <span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fifa</span>, and <span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kings_county</span>.
We only extract the eigenvalues here, and later in <a href="#S11" title="11 Implications on the Scaling Law ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> we will investigate the eigenfunctions.
We use KNN with <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=60</annotation></semantics></math> as context, where <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(x^{\prime}|x)=K^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(x^{\prime}|x)=K^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-nearest neighbor of <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
For this context, we can exactly compute <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus we can obtain the exact eigenvalues (ground truth) using kernel PCA.
Meanwhile, we pretrain <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> with one of the variational objectives using a random subset of <math id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> samples, and estimate the eigenvalues using the post-hoc approach.
Then, we compare the estimation with the ground truth.</p>
</div>
