stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000"><path d="M 299.35 28.46 h 55.26 v 55.12 h -55.26 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 303.5 39.49)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 49.58)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 8.26)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke-width="1.2pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(0.83,0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 5.91 0 L 11.81 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 13.47 0) translate(-0.28,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.04 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="20.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S11.F7.pic1.32" class="ltx_text">512</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 24.79)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke-width="1.2pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(0.83,0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 5.91 0 L 11.81 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 13.47 0) translate(-0.28,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.04 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="27.67"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S11.F7.pic1.33" class="ltx_text">1024</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 41.32)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke-width="1.2pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(0.83,0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 5.91 0 L 11.81 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 13.47 0) translate(-0.28,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.04 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="27.67"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S11.F7.pic1.34" class="ltx_text">2048</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 433.35 -83.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.81em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="38.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S11.F7.pic1.35" class="ltx_text">Width</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Alignment between the learned representation and the top-<math id="S11.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S11.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the <span id="S11.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span> dataset. Solid curves: CCA. Dashed curves: mutual KNN. Depth here means the number of hidden layers.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S11.F7" title="In Setup. ‣ 11 Implications on the Scaling Law ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> plots the alignment between the two encoders while varying the depth and width of the neural network.
we can see that when the depth and width are chosen correctly, the CCA can be as high as 0.9, and the mutual KNN can be over 0.8.
Note that these alignment metric values are very high.
For example, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, the mutual KNN metric value is usually below 0.2.
Hence, the representation learned by the neural network is highly aligned with the top-<math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The top plot studies neural networks with increasing widths.
We can see that when the neural network is not so wide, increasing the width will make the alignment higher.
However, once the neural network is wide enough, further increasing the width might have a negative effect.
For example, when the depth is 3, the alignment is the highest when the width is 512, and the alignment becomes lower when the network is wider than 512.
Since increasing the width can only make the function class of <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> larger, this phenomenon is not due to the expressivity of the neural network.
We hypothesize that this is because optimizing a larger model is harder.
Consequently, with the same number of pretraining steps, a larger model will be farther away from the minima, and the alignment decreases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The bottom plot studies neural networks with increasing depths, and the observation is similar.
When the network is shallow, increasing the depth makes the alignment higher.
However, once the network is deep enough, further increasing the depth might have a negative effect.
We also observe from the bottom plot that a width-512 network has higher alignment than widths 1024 and 2048.
In addition, the alignment cannot reach 1.
This is because the model is non-convex, so the real optima (the precise top-<math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace) cannot be found by gradient methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, we draw two conclusions from this experiment:
(i) the representation learned by a large neural network is highly aligned with the top-<math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions;
(ii) once the neural network is wide and deep enough, further increasing its size will not make the alignment higher, and might even have a negative effect.
Hence, we put forward the following argument about the scaling law:
Once the model is large enough such that <math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is already highly aligned with the top-<math id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions, further increasing the model size inevitably yields diminishing returns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When the model is already large enough, a better context is necessary for further improvement.
The next part of this thesis studies how to obtain a better context.
There are two questions that need to be addressed.
First, what context is good and what context is “better”?
In particular, if we want the representation to be transferable to a wide variety of downstream tasks, then how should we evaluate the representation without testing it on a specific task?
Second, how to obtain better contexts?
Creating new contexts from scratch is obviously difficult, so are there easier options?
These questions will be addressed in the following two chapters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Code and data availability.</h4>

<div id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The code for the experiment in this section can be found at <a href="https://1drv.ms/u/c/ea9fe908498c8b82/EWb_t4e-27VKsoSTWV6J_yQBUFsuWSAXbRHJK4GoUEynjw?e=r4ROdU" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">https://1drv.ms/u/c/ea9fe908498c8b82/EWb_t4e-27VKsoSTWV6J_yQBUFsuWSAXbRHJK4GoUEynjw?e=r4ROdU</a>.
The data can be downloaded from OpenML.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="chapter3" class="ltx_chapter">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_chapter"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_chapter">Chapter 3 </span>Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture</h2>

<div id="chapter3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This chapter focuses on the evaluation of encoders and contexts.
How to tell if an encoder is good or not?
There are two methods as summarized in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Sec. 4]</cite>.
The first method is extrinsic evaluation, where we choose a specific downstream task, fit a predictor on top of the encoder, and use the performance of that predictor as the evaluation of the encoder.
In practice, this performance is ultimately what we care about.
The second method is intrinsic evaluation, which is independent of any downstream task.
Intrinsic evaluation is more useful in the analysis of pretraining for two reasons:
(i) We would like the encoder to be transferable to a wide variety of downstream tasks, so only evaluating it on one task is usually insufficient;
(ii) Foundation models are often used on tasks they are not designed for, so we cannot assume that we know all the tasks at pretrain time.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Obviously, no encoder is good for all tasks.
For any encoder, one can adversarially construct a task for which the encoder is poor.
However, such tasks are not what we care about.
For example, in NLP, downstream tasks that we care about include sentiment analysis, sentence completion, translation, question answer, etc.
Counting how many words in a paragraph contain the letter "e" is a valid task, and large language models are not really good at it (see <a href="#S11.F8" title="In Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), but this task is not one that we would usually care about.
Therefore, we need to define a class of relevant tasks, and the intrinsic evaluation of the encoder is conducted on this class.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S11.F8" class="ltx_figure"><pre id="S11.F8.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
User: Count how many words in the following paragraph
contains the letter "e":
Overall, although there does not ... the proposed metric
is useful for choosing hyperparameters and comparing
contexts in practice.

Claude 3.7 Sonnet: I’ll count the words containing the
letter "e" in the paragraph you provided. Let me go
through it word by word:
1. "Overall" - contains "e"
...
58. "and" - no "e"
59. "comparing" - contains "e"
60. "contexts" - contains "e"
61. "in" - no "e"
62. "practice" - contains "e"
Counting the words with the letter "e": 42 words out
of 62 total words contain the letter "e".
</pre>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>An example where the LLM Claude 3.7 Sonnet (as of April 14, 2025) makes a mistake on a task that is not quite relevant to NLP. Line 59 is wrong because “comparing” does not contain the letter “e”. The final answer is also wrong.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="chapter3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">How to define such a class of relevant tasks at pretrain time?
The only information we have at pretrain time is the context, and the prior knowledge that the context should be useful for learning an encoder for any task of interest.
Note that a downstream task need not to “look similar” to the context; for example, a BERT trained with masked token prediction can be used for sentiment analysis, though these two tasks look quite different.
Thus, we need a quantitative description of the usefulness of a context for a task, for which we define the <span id="chapter3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compatibility</span> between a context and a task.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Once we define the class of compatible tasks, we can prove that the encoder that learns the contexture is optimal for this class of tasks, in the sense that it minimizes the worst-case approximation error.
On the other hand, if the task is not compatible, then it is likely that the encoder will have a bad performance.
This explains why LLMs are so powerful that they can perform well on various tasks, but they struggle with easy tasks such as answering factual questions, where they often produce seemingly correct but completely wrong answers, a phenomenon known as hallucination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>.
In short, answering factual questions is not compatible with the context of next token prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The significance of this result is that it reduces the problem of pretraining to the problem of finding a good context.
Once we have the context, we can use any variational objective in the last chapter, and the resulting foundation model is guaranteed to have the optimal performance if it can learn the contexture well.
We will also discuss how to intrinsically evaluate an arbitrary encoder, which depends on two key concepts—the induced RKHS and the ratio trace.
Thus, even if the foundation model does not learn the contexture, we are able to predict its performance on compatible tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we study what contexts are good contexts.
The key finding is that a good context should have a moderate association between <math id="chapter3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="chapter3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the decay rate of the singular values is neither too fast nor too slow.
Qualitatively, if the association is too weak, then there will be very few tasks that are compatible with the context, so the resulting encoder will not be transferable to a wide range of tasks.
On the other hand, if the association is too strong, then the sample complexity of learning the contexture and the downstream predictor will be very high.
Quantitatively, we propose a metric that measures the usefulness of a context.
This metric only depends on the singular values, so it can be efficiently estimated using the post-hoc approach described in <a href="#S10" title="10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
Experiments show that the metric correlates well with the actual performance of the encoder on real datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S12" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">12 </span>Compatibility, Optimality of Contexture</h3>

<div id="S12.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A downstream task is represented by a <span id="S12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">target function</span>.
Most downstream tasks, such as prediction, clustering, and segmentation, can be associated with a target function <math id="S12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, multi-class classification can be associated with multiple one-vs-all labeling functions.
After <math id="S12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is pretrained, and the training samples of the downstream task are revealed, there are a number of ways to use <math id="S12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, such as fitting a small neural network on top, using a kernel method, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), etc.
This thesis focuses on the simplest way called a <span id="S12.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">linear probe</span>, where one fits the downstream data with a linear predictor on top of <math id="S12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, the downstream predictor is given by <math id="S12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}(x)={\bm{W}}\Phi(x)+{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}(x)={\bm{W}}\Phi(x)+{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be arbitrarily chosen, the mean and variance of <math id="S12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> have no impact on the difficulty of learning <math id="S12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, our definition of compatibility should be independent of them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given a context <math id="S12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that a task is compatible with it, if the information the context provides makes it easier to learn a predictor for the task.
Formally, consider a training set for supervised learning <math id="S12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\{(a_{i},y_{i})\right\}}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\{(a_{i},y_{i})\right\}}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=f^{*}(x_{i})+\xi_{i}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=f^{*}(x_{i})+\xi_{i}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for some small random noise <math id="S12.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this dataset, the original inputs <math id="S12.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown, and only one <math id="S12.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{i}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is provided for each <math id="S12.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
The most straightforward method of learning a predictor for this task is as follows:
we first fit a predictor <math id="S12.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{g}:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}:{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on this dataset, and then convert it to <math id="S12.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S12.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}=T_{P^{+}}\hat{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}=T_{P^{+}}\hat{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming that we have <math id="S12.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>-access to the context.
<math id="S12.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be compatible with the context if this method works well, for which two conditions are necessary:</p>
<ol id="S12.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S12.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a <math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}=T_{P^{+}}g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}=T_{P^{+}}g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S12.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The variance of <math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on <math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}[g^{*}(A)|x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}[g^{*}(A)|x]</annotation></semantics></math>, is low on average.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S12.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Condition (i) says that the labels <math id="S12.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by a function in the range of <math id="S12.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, up to the small noise <math id="S12.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Condition (ii) ensures that <math id="S12.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}(a_{i})\approx f^{*}(x_{i})\approx y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}(a_{i})\approx f^{*}(x_{i})\approx y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; without this condition, the <math id="S12.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}</annotation></semantics></math> fit on <math id="S12.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\{(a_{i},y_{i})\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\{(a_{i},y_{i})\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot approximate <math id="S12.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The compatibility defined below is based on the above insights.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compatibility</span> with <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}\neq{\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}\neq{\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S12.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(f,P^{+})=\max_{g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})},g\neq{\bm{0}}}\frac{{\left\langle\tilde{f},T_{P^{+}}g\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}}{{\left\|\tilde{f}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left\|g\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}}\in[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(f,P^{+})=\max_{g\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})},g\neq{\bm{0}}}\frac{{\left\langle\tilde{f},T_{P^{+}}g\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}}{{\left\|\tilde{f}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left\|g\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}}\in[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The compatibility is defined this way so that it is independent of the mean and variance of <math id="S12.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here is a formula for the compatibility of any <math id="S12.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S12.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For simplicity, let <math id="S12.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> has mean zero.
Let <math id="S12.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}=\underset{i\geq 1}{\sum}u_{i}\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">≥</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}=\underset{i\geq 1}{\sum}u_{i}\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}=\underset{i\geq 1}{\sum}v_{i}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">≥</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}=\underset{i\geq 1}{\sum}v_{i}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S12.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(f^{*},P^{+})=\underset{v_{i}}{\max}\frac{\sum_{i}s_{i}u_{i}v_{i}}{\sqrt{\sum_{i}u_{i}^{2}}\sqrt{\sum_{i}v_{i}^{2}}}=\sqrt{\frac{\sum s_{i}^{2}u_{i}^{2}}{\sum u_{i}^{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>max</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">v</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">u</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mstyle></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(f^{*},P^{+})=\underset{v_{i}}{\max}\frac{\sum_{i}s_{i}u_{i}v_{i}}{\sqrt{\sum_{i}u_{i}^{2}}\sqrt{\sum_{i}v_{i}^{2}}}=\sqrt{\frac{\sum s_{i}^{2}u_{i}^{2}}{\sum u_{i}^{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math> by Cauchy-Schwarz inequality (the maximum is attained when <math id="S12.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\propto s_{i}u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\propto s_{i}u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>).
To estimate <math id="S12.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(f^{*},P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(f^{*},P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S12.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the most straightforward way is to find the <math id="S12.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> ths maximizes the fraction on the right-hand side.
This is much more efficient than training a <math id="S12.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Under this definition, the class of <math id="S12.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-compatible tasks is defined as</p>
<table id="S12.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})={\left\{f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}:\rho(f,P^{+})\geq 1-\epsilon\right\}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})={\left\{f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}:\rho(f,P^{+})\geq 1-\epsilon\right\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p6.2" class="ltx_p">for any <math id="S12.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S12.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is small, this is a class of compatible downstream tasks, and it can be shown that this class satisfies the two conditions aforementioned:</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter9.A1" title="Appendix 9.A Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 9 Proofs for Chapter 3 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.A</span></a>)<span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{A}})}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(x)=\mathbb{E}[g^{*}(A)|x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(x)=\mathbb{E}[g^{*}(A)|x]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S12.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A,A^{\prime}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|X)}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left(g^{*}(A)-g^{*}(A^{\prime})\right)}^{2}\right]}\leq 4\epsilon{\left\|g^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.710em">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">+</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\mathbb{E}}\;\underset{A,A^{\prime}\sim P^{+}(\cdot|X)}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left(g^{*}(A)-g^{*}(A^{\prime})\right)}^{2}\right]}\leq 4\epsilon{\left\|g^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we show why an encoder that learns the contexture is optimal.
First, we need to define “optimal”, which involves the evaluation of an encoder.
The ultimate evaluation of <math id="S12.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the performance of the downstream predictor.
Let <math id="S12.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)={\bm{W}}\Phi(x)+{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)={\bm{W}}\Phi(x)+{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the downstream linear probe.
The performance of <math id="S12.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be measured by the mean squared error (MSE):</p>
<table id="S12.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(f,f^{*})={\left\|f-f^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[(f(X)-f^{*}(X))^{2}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(f,f^{*})={\left\|f-f^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}=\mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[(f(X)-f^{*}(X))^{2}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection of <math id="S12.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> onto the linear space spanned by <math id="S12.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, when using a linear probe, the above error can be decomposed as</p>
<table id="S12.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(f,f^{*})=\underbrace{{\left\|f_{\Phi}-f^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}}_{\textrm{Approximation error}}+\underbrace{{\left\|f-f_{\Phi}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}}_{\textrm{Estimation error}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Approximation error</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Estimation error</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(f,f^{*})=\underbrace{{\left\|f_{\Phi}-f^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}}_{\textrm{Approximation error}}+\underbrace{{\left\|f-f_{\Phi}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}}_{\textrm{Estimation error}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S12.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The embedding dimension <math id="S12.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> controls the trade-off between the two errors.
If <math id="S12.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is larger, then the span of <math id="S12.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> will become larger, so the approximation error will be lower;
meanwhile, the downstream sample complexity will be higher, so the estimation error will increase.
There are two ways in which <math id="S12.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> affects the final prediction error.
First, the distance from <math id="S12.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to the span of <math id="S12.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> decides the approximation error.
Second, the smoothness of <math id="S12.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> affects the sample complexity of both pretraining and downstream.
The second part will be studied in <a href="#chapter5" title="Chapter 5 Statistical Learning Bounds for Representation Learning ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Chapter</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>,
and this chapter evaluates <math id="S12.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> by the approximation error.
More specifically, <math id="S12.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated on the class of compatible tasks <math id="S12.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> by its worst-case approximation error.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}\subset{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}\subset{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> be a function class where <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{\mathcal{F}}\Rightarrow\alpha f\in{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{\mathcal{F}}\Rightarrow\alpha f\in{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">worst-case approximation error</span> of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S12.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S12.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max_{f\in{\mathcal{F}}(P^{+}),\;{\left\|f\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=1}\;\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi,f\right)};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>err</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max_{f\in{\mathcal{F}}(P^{+}),\;{\left\|f\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=1}\;\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi,f\right)};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S12.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{where}\quad\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi,f\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{where}\quad\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi,f\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S12.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\Phi}{\left[\min_{{\bm{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d},\;b\in\mathbb{R}}\;{\left\|{\bm{w}}^{\top}\Phi+b-f\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}^{2}\right]}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\Phi}{\left[\min_{{\bm{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d},\;b\in\mathbb{R}}\;{\left\|{\bm{w}}^{\top}\Phi+b-f\right\|}_{P_{\mathcal{X}}}^{2}\right]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S12.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S12.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is taken over the randomness of <math id="S12.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S12.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is randomized, one first samples a deterministic <math id="S12.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> from the distribution, and then fits <math id="S12.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> accordingly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is one of the main results of the contexture theory.
It says two things.
First, if we know a priori the downstream task is compatible, then learning the contexture is the optimal thing to do, because it minimizes the worst-case approximation error.
Second, what if the task is incompatible?
We cannot argue that no encoder works for an incompatible task.
In a hypothetical scenario, if an oracle tells us the target function <math id="S12.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in advance, then we can set <math id="S12.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}=f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}=f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve perfect performance.
What we can argue is that for any low compatibility level and any encoder <math id="S12.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="S12.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on that level such that <math id="S12.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is poor for <math id="S12.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter9.A2" title="Appendix 9.B Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 9 Proofs for Chapter 3 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.B</span></a>)<span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon\leq s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon\leq s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, among all <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=[\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=[\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> , <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it learns the contexture of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The error is given by</span></p>
<table id="S12.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d},\;\phi_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}}\;\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})\right)}=\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-{\epsilon})^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mtext>err</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d},\;\phi_{i}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}}\;\textnormal{err}{\left(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})\right)}=\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-{\epsilon})^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conversely, for any <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional encoder <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(f,P^{+})=1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(f,P^{+})=1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(\Phi,f)\geq\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-{\epsilon})^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(\Phi,f)\geq\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-{\epsilon})^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S13" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">13 </span>Intrinsic Evaluation of an Arbitrary Encoder</h3>

<div id="S13.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a context that is compatible with the task, the encoder that learns the contexture is optimal.
Now what about an arbitrary encoder <math id="S13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>? Is it possible to bound its worst-case approximation error on the class of compatible tasks?
To derive such a bound, two key objects are necessary: the induced RKHS and the ratio trace.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the range of <math id="S13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{P^{+}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{P^{+}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S13.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T_{P^{+}}^{*})={\left\{T_{P^{+}}^{*}f\;\middle|\;f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T_{P^{+}}^{*})={\left\{T_{P^{+}}^{*}f\;\middle|\;f\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">induced RKHS</span> of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the Hilbert space <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T_{P^{+}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T_{P^{+}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> with the inner product given by <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{1},T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{2}\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}={\left\langle f_{1},f_{2}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{1},T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{2}\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}={\left\langle f_{1},f_{2}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S13.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative formula is that for any <math id="S13.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1},h_{2}\in{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1},h_{2}\in{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S13.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}=\sum u_{i}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}=\sum u_{i}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{2}=\sum v_{i}\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{2}=\sum v_{i}\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is <math id="S13.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle h_{1},h_{2}\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}=\sum\frac{u_{i}v_{i}}{s_{i}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle h_{1},h_{2}\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}=\sum\frac{u_{i}v_{i}}{s_{i}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.6</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The induced RKHS <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> has the following properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S13.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S13.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S13.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S13.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{A}^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{A}^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S13.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the reproducing kernel, such that </span><math id="S13.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(a)={\left\langle h,{k_{A}^{+}}(a,\cdot)\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(a)={\left\langle h,{k_{A}^{+}}(a,\cdot)\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S13.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S13.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S13.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S13.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S13.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S13.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S13.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is isometric to </span><math id="S13.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textnormal{span}}{\left\{\mu_{i}:s_{i}&gt;0\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>span</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textnormal{span}}{\left\{\mu_{i}:s_{i}&gt;0\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S13.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, which is a subspace of </span><math id="S13.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S13.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S13.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S13.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S13.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S13.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is equivalent to </span><math id="S13.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S13.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfying the following </span><span id="S13.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">isometry property</span><span id="S13.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:</span></p>
<table id="S13.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\epsilon){\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}\leq{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\leq{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\epsilon){\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}\leq{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\leq{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S13.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
For any <math id="S13.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S13.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=T_{P^{+}}^{*}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=T_{P^{+}}^{*}f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\sum u_{i}\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\sum u_{i}\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, by <a href="#chapter1.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 1.6. ‣ 3 Spectral Properties of a Context ‣ Chapter 1 Introduction to the Contexture Theory ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> we have</p>
<table id="S13.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle h,{k_{A}^{+}}(a,\cdot)\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}={\left\langle\sum s_{i}u_{i}\nu_{i},\sum s_{i}^{2}\nu_{i}(a)\nu_{i}\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}=\sum s_{i}u_{i}\nu_{i}(a)=h(a)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle h,{k_{A}^{+}}(a,\cdot)\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}={\left\langle\sum s_{i}u_{i}\nu_{i},\sum s_{i}^{2}\nu_{i}(a)\nu_{i}\right\rangle}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}=\sum s_{i}u_{i}\nu_{i}(a)=h(a),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.p4.2" class="ltx_p">which proves (i). (ii) is obvious. Regarding (iii), recall that <math id="S13.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}=\sum u_{i}\mu_{i}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}=\sum u_{i}\mu_{i}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S13.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\geq 1}s_{i}^{2}u_{i}^{2}\geq(1-\epsilon)^{2}\sum_{i\geq 1}u_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\geq 1}s_{i}^{2}u_{i}^{2}\geq(1-\epsilon)^{2}\sum_{i\geq 1}u_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and this is <math id="S13.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\geq(1-\epsilon){\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\geq(1-\epsilon){\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Meanwhile, it is obvious that <math id="S13.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\leq{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\leq{\left\|\tilde{h}^{*}\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> always holds.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.7</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define covariance matrices <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}[T_{P^{+}}^{*}\Phi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}[T_{P^{+}}^{*}\Phi]</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible, then the <span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ratio trace</span> of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})=\textnormal{RT}(\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d};P^{+}):=\Tr({\bm{C}}_{\Phi}^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{\Phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})=\textnormal{RT}(\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d};P^{+}):=\Tr({\bm{C}}_{\Phi}^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{\Phi})</annotation></semantics></math>;
otherwise, let <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\prime}={\left[\phi_{i_{1}},\cdots,\phi_{i_{t}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\prime}={\left[\phi_{i_{1}},\cdots,\phi_{i_{t}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> be the maximal linearly independent subset of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left[\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left[\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the ratio trace of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> the same as the ratio trace of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S13.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The ratio trace of any <math id="S13.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> essentially measures how well <math id="S13.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is aligned with the contexture of <math id="S13.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Multiplying <math id="S13.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> by any invertible matrix does not change its ratio trace.
The matrices <math id="S13.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> here are the same as in <a href="#S10" title="10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
If <math id="S13.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> learns the contexture, then its ratio trace is <math id="S13.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be easily shown by setting <math id="S13.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}=\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}=\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact, this is the maximum ratio trace of any <math id="S13.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.8</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthonormal and all have zero mean. Then, we have</span></p>
<table id="S13.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}\phi_{1}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+\cdots+{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}\phi_{d}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}\phi_{1}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+\cdots+{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}\phi_{d}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S13.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
Let <math id="S13.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{i}=\sum_{j\geq 1}q_{ij}\mu_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{i}=\sum_{j\geq 1}q_{ij}\mu_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S13.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S13.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}}=(q_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}}=(q_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> is a matrix with <math id="S13.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> orthonormal rows and infinitely many columns.
It is easy to see that the left-hand side is equal to <math id="S13.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tr({\bm{Q}}{\bm{D}}{\bm{Q}}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tr({\bm{Q}}{\bm{D}}{\bm{Q}}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S13.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{D}}={\textnormal{diag}}{\left\{s_{1}^{2},s_{2}^{2},\cdots\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{D}}={\textnormal{diag}}{\left\{s_{1}^{2},s_{2}^{2},\cdots\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S13.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{q}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{q}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S13.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of <math id="S13.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="S13.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, there is <math id="S13.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{j}{\bm{q}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{q}}_{i}\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{j}{\bm{q}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{q}}_{i}\leq j</annotation></semantics></math>; and for any <math id="S13.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;d</annotation></semantics></math>, there is <math id="S13.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{j}{\bm{q}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{q}}_{i}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{j}{\bm{q}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{q}}_{i}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, using Abel transformation, we have</p>
<table id="S13.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Tr({\bm{Q}}{\bm{D}}{\bm{Q}}^{\top})=\Tr({\bm{D}}{\bm{Q}}^{\top}{\bm{Q}})=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}s_{j}^{2}{\bm{q}}_{j}^{\top}{\bm{q}}_{j}=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{j}{\bm{q}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{q}}_{i}\right)}{\left(s_{j}^{2}-s_{j+1}^{2}\right)}\leq\sum_{j=1}^{d}s_{j}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒒</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Tr({\bm{Q}}{\bm{D}}{\bm{Q}}^{\top})=\Tr({\bm{D}}{\bm{Q}}^{\top}{\bm{Q}})=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}s_{j}^{2}{\bm{q}}_{j}^{\top}{\bm{q}}_{j}=\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{j}{\bm{q}}_{i}^{\top}{\bm{q}}_{i}\right)}{\left(s_{j}^{2}-s_{j+1}^{2}\right)}\leq\sum_{j=1}^{d}s_{j}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.p6.2" class="ltx_p">as desired.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The ratio trace induces a key quantity in the approximation error bound called the trace gap, which reflects the gap between <math id="S13.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and the top-<math id="S13.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions.
The larger the trace gap is, the larger the approximation error will be.
A simple definition is <math id="S13.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d+1}^{2}-\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d+1}^{2}-\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose lower bound <math id="S13.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{d+1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{d+1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be achieved by the top-<math id="S13.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions, the optimal encoder.
However, there is an issue with this definition.
For example, consider an encoder with <math id="S13.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1000</annotation></semantics></math>.
It learns the top-<math id="S13.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions, but the other <math id="S13.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="990" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>990</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">990</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions are complete noise that has zero contribution to <math id="S13.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The approximation error of this encoder should be no higher than that of the top-<math id="S13.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> singular functions, because adding more dimensions will never make the approximation error higher.
However, if <math id="S13.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> becomes larger and <math id="S13.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> stays the same, then <math id="S13.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d+1}^{2}-\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d+1}^{2}-\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> will become larger, so this quantity does not correlate with the approximation error in this scenario.
The following definition fixes this issue.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.9</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any linearly independent <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}\in{L^{2}(P_{\mathcal{X}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=[f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=[f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{F}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[F]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{F}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[F]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{F}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}[F]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{F}=\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}[F]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">trace gap</span> of <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S13.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+}):=\inf_{d^{\prime}\leq d}\;\inf_{f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}}\;{\left\{s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d^{\prime}+1}^{2}-\Tr({\bm{C}}_{F}^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{F})\right\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>TG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">inf</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+}):=\inf_{d^{\prime}\leq d}\;\inf_{f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}}\;{\left\{s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d^{\prime}+1}^{2}-\Tr({\bm{C}}_{F}^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{F})\right\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S13.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Obviously, this definition of trace gap is upper bounded by <math id="S13.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d+1}^{2}-\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>RT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d+1}^{2}-\textnormal{RT}(\Phi;P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>.
It solves the issue in the previous example, because having completely noisy dimensions does not affect the trace gap.
The following result bounds the approximation error.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.10</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})&lt;s_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>TG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})&lt;s_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;1-s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;1-s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S13.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))\leq\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}+s_{1}\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})}{s_{1}^{2}-\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>TG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))\leq\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}+s_{1}\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})}{s_{1}^{2}-\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.11</span></span><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This bound is fairly tight. If <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> learns the contexture, then by <a href="#chapter3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Proof in ). ‣ 12 Compatibility, Optimality of Contexture ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> we have <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))=\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))=\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})=s_{d+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>TG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})=s_{d+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Compared to this exact formula, the above upper bound only has an extra <math id="chapter3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>TG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> term in the numerator.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S13.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
Let <math id="S13.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the functions that minimize <math id="S13.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d^{\prime}+1}^{2}-\Tr({\bm{C}}_{F}^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d^{\prime}+1}^{2}-\Tr({\bm{C}}_{F}^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of generality, assume that <math id="S13.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> have zero mean and are orthonormal. Let <math id="S13.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}={\textnormal{span}}{\left\{f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>span</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}={\textnormal{span}}{\left\{f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S13.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}={\textnormal{span}}{\left\{T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{1},\cdots,T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{d^{\prime}}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>span</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}={\textnormal{span}}{\left\{T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{1},\cdots,T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{d^{\prime}}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S13.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S13.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|f\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|f\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S13.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=T_{P^{+}}^{*}f\in{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=T_{P^{+}}^{*}f\in{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S13.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{{\mathcal{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{{\mathcal{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection of <math id="S13.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S13.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S13.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>err</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}(\Phi;{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+}))</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by <math id="S13.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|f-f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|f-f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to show that <math id="S13.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|f-f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|f-f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded by the right-hand side.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}={\left\|f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}={\left\|f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S13.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{2}={\left\|f-f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{2}={\left\|f-f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S13.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are non-negative.
Then, <math id="S13.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}={\left\|f\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}=1={\left\|h\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2}={\left\|f\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}=1={\left\|h\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}_{P^{+}}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The isometry property says that <math id="S13.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\epsilon)^{2}(\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})\leq{\left\|h\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\epsilon)^{2}(\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})\leq{\left\|h\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S13.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f-f_{{\mathcal{F}}}=\beta f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f-f_{{\mathcal{F}}}=\beta f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S13.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|f_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|f_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S13.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{{\mathcal{F}}}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}h_{{\mathcal{F}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{{\mathcal{F}}}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}h_{{\mathcal{F}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{0}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{0}=T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we have <math id="S13.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|h_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq s_{1}^{2}{\left\|f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}=s_{1}^{2}\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|h_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq s_{1}^{2}{\left\|f_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}^{2}=s_{1}^{2}\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Meanwhile, since <math id="S13.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal to <math id="S13.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1},\cdots,f_{d^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, by <a href="#chapter3.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 3.8. ‣ 13 Intrinsic Evaluation of an Arbitrary Encoder ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> we have <math id="S13.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{1}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+\cdots+{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{d^{\prime}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d^{\prime}+1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{1}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+\cdots+{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{d^{\prime}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d^{\prime}+1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S13.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d^{\prime}+1}^{2}-\Tr({\bm{C}}_{F}^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{F}^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|T_{P^{+}}^{*}f_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq s_{1}^{2}+\cdots+s_{d^{\prime}+1}^{2}-\Tr({\bm{C}}_{F}^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{F}^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S13.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>TG</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\textnormal{TG}(\Phi;P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have</p>
<table id="S13.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|h\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}={\left\|h_{{\mathcal{F}}}+\beta h_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq{\left\|h_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+\beta^{2}{\left\|h_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+2\beta{\left\|h_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left\|h_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\leq s_{1}^{2}\alpha^{2}+\tau^{2}\beta^{2}+2s_{1}\tau\alpha\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|h\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}={\left\|h_{{\mathcal{F}}}+\beta h_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}\leq{\left\|h_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+\beta^{2}{\left\|h_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}^{2}+2\beta{\left\|h_{{\mathcal{F}}}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}{\left\|h_{0}\right\|}_{{P_{\mathcal{A}}}}\leq s_{1}^{2}\alpha^{2}+\tau^{2}\beta^{2}+2s_{1}\tau\alpha\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.p10.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, we have <math id="S13.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\epsilon)^{2}(\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})\leq s_{1}^{2}\alpha^{2}+\tau^{2}\beta^{2}+2s_{1}\tau\alpha\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\epsilon)^{2}(\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})\leq s_{1}^{2}\alpha^{2}+\tau^{2}\beta^{2}+2s_{1}\tau\alpha\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S13.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{1}^{2}-\tau^{2})\beta^{2}\leq[s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}](\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})+2s_{1}\tau\alpha\beta\leq[s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}+s_{1}\tau](\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{1}^{2}-\tau^{2})\beta^{2}\leq[s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}](\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})+2s_{1}\tau\alpha\beta\leq[s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}+s_{1}\tau](\alpha^{2}+\beta^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<section id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Connection to Fisher discriminant analysis.</h4>

<div id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fisher discriminant analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">106</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>]</cite>, or more generally linear discriminant analysis (LDA), is a classical method of learning linear classifiers in statistics.
Here we show that Fisher discriminant analysis has a strong connection to the contexture theory.
Suppose <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fisher discriminant analysis defines the following <span id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">between-class covariance matrix</span> <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{S}}_{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{X}}}\times d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{S}}_{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{X}}}\times d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">within-class covariance matrix</span> <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{S}}_{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{X}}}\times d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{S}}_{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{{\mathcal{X}}}\times d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S13.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S13.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{S}}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{S}}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\iint{\left\{{\left(\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a_{1}\right]}-\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a_{2}\right]}\right)}{\left(\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a_{1}\right]}-\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a_{2}\right]}\right)}^{\top}\right\}};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∬</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\iint{\left\{{\left(\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a_{1}\right]}-\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a_{2}\right]}\right)}{\left(\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a_{1}\right]}-\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a_{2}\right]}\right)}^{\top}\right\}};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S13.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{S}}_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{S}}_{W}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\mathbb{E}_{P^{+}}{\left[{\left(X-\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}\right)}{\left(X-\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}\right)}^{\top}\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\mathbb{E}_{P^{+}}{\left[{\left(X-\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}\right)}{\left(X-\mathbb{E}{\left[X\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}\right)}^{\top}\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the original formulation of Fisher discriminant analysis, <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the label of <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we extend it to a general context variable.
Consider a linear encoder <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x)={\bm{W}}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x)={\bm{W}}x</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, one solves the following optimization problem to find <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S13.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}}{\text{maximize}}\;J({\bm{W}})=\Tr{\left[{\left({\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{B}{\bm{W}}^{\top}\right)}{\left({\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{W}{\bm{W}}^{\top}\right)}^{-1}\right]}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{W}{\bm{W}}^{\top}\text{ is invertible}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>maximize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝑾</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.710em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is invertible</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{{\mathcal{X}}}}}{\text{maximize}}\;J({\bm{W}})=\Tr{\left[{\left({\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{B}{\bm{W}}^{\top}\right)}{\left({\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{W}{\bm{W}}^{\top}\right)}^{-1}\right]}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{W}{\bm{W}}^{\top}\text{ is invertible}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J({\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J({\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fisher discriminant</span>.
Define <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi(a)=\mathbb{E}_{P^{+}}[{\bm{W}}X|A=a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(a)=\mathbb{E}_{P^{+}}[{\bm{W}}X|A=a]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we can see that</p>
<table id="S13.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S13.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{B}{\bm{W}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{B}{\bm{W}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\iint{\left(\Psi(a_{1})-\Psi(a_{2})\right)}{\left(\Psi(a_{1})-\Psi(a_{2})\right)}^{\top}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a_{1})d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a_{2});" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∬</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\iint{\left(\Psi(a_{1})-\Psi(a_{2})\right)}{\left(\Psi(a_{1})-\Psi(a_{2})\right)}^{\top}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a_{1})d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a_{2});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S13.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{W}{\bm{W}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{W}{\bm{W}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\mathbb{E}_{P^{+}}{\left[{\left(\Phi(X)-\Psi(a)\right)}{\left(\Phi(X)-\Psi(a)\right)}^{\top}\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\mathbb{E}_{P^{+}}{\left[{\left(\Phi(X)-\Psi(a)\right)}{\left(\Phi(X)-\Psi(a)\right)}^{\top}\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}=\mathbb{E}[\tilde{\Phi}(X)\tilde{\Phi}(X)^{\top}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}_{\Phi}=\mathbb{E}[\tilde{\Phi}(X)\tilde{\Phi}(X)^{\top}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}=\mathbb{E}[\tilde{\Psi}(A)\tilde{\Psi}(A)^{\top}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}=\mathbb{E}[\tilde{\Psi}(A)\tilde{\Psi}(A)^{\top}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we have</p>
<table id="S13.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S13.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{B}{\bm{W}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{B}{\bm{W}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2{\left\{\mathbb{E}{\left[\Psi(A)\Psi(A)^{\top}\right]}-\bar{\Psi}\bar{\Psi}^{\top}\right\}}=2\mathbb{E}{\left[\tilde{\Psi}(A)\tilde{\Psi}(A)^{\top}\right]}=2{\bm{B}}_{\Phi};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2{\left\{\mathbb{E}{\left[\Psi(A)\Psi(A)^{\top}\right]}-\bar{\Psi}\bar{\Psi}^{\top}\right\}}=2\mathbb{E}{\left[\tilde{\Psi}(A)\tilde{\Psi}(A)^{\top}\right]}=2{\bm{B}}_{\Phi};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S13.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{W}{\bm{W}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{W}}{\bm{S}}_{W}{\bm{W}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int\mathbb{E}_{P^{+}}{\left[\Phi(X)\Phi(X)^{\top}-\Psi(a)\Psi(a)^{\top}\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int\mathbb{E}_{P^{+}}{\left[\Phi(X)\Phi(X)^{\top}-\Psi(a)\Psi(a)^{\top}\;\middle|\;A=a\right]}d{P_{\mathcal{A}}}(a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S13.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}{\left[\Phi(X)\Phi(X)^{\top}\right]}-\mathbb{E}{\left[\Psi(A)\Psi(A)^{\top}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}{\left[\Phi(X)\Phi(X)^{\top}\right]}-\mathbb{E}{\left[\Psi(A)\Psi(A)^{\top}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S13.Ex33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S13.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}{\left[\tilde{\Phi}(X)\tilde{\Phi}(X)^{\top}\right]}-\mathbb{E}{\left[\tilde{\Psi}(A)\tilde{\Psi}(A)^{\top}\right]}={\bm{C}}_{\Phi}-{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}{\left[\tilde{\Phi}(X)\tilde{\Phi}(X)^{\top}\right]}-\mathbb{E}{\left[\tilde{\Psi}(A)\tilde{\Psi}(A)^{\top}\right]}={\bm{C}}_{\Phi}-{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">Therefore, <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J({\bm{W}})=2\Tr[({\bm{C}}_{\Phi}-{\bm{B}}_{\Phi})^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J({\bm{W}})=2\Tr[({\bm{C}}_{\Phi}-{\bm{B}}_{\Phi})^{-1}{\bm{B}}_{\Phi}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is very similar to the ratio trace defined in <a href="#chapter3.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 3.7. ‣ 13 Intrinsic Evaluation of an Arbitrary Encoder ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>.
Recall that an encoder that learns the contexture maximizes the ratio trace.
A well-known result is that <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J({\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J({\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> is maximized when <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the top-<math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvectors of <math id="S13.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{S}}_{W}^{-1}{\bm{S}}_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑺</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{S}}_{W}^{-1}{\bm{S}}_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, Fisher discriminant analysis is almost equivalent to contexture learning under the constraint that the encoder must be linear.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S14" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">14 </span>Evaluating Context Usefulness</h3>

<div id="S14.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous chapter argued that better contexts are essential to further improve foundation models, but how to create better contexts is a challenging open problem.
This section studies an easier problem—how to evaluate a context before pretraining.
We say that a context is <span id="S14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">useful</span> for a downstream task, if it can lead to an encoder with good performance on this task.
However, since we might not know the task at pretrain time, we can only predict if the context is useful <span id="S14.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in general</span>.
Solving this problem is a prerequisite, because if we cannot even decide whether a context is good or not, then there will be no way for us to create better contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluating a context is more difficult than evaluating an encoder.
When evaluating an encoder, we can assume that the task is known to be compatible with the context.
However, when evaluating a context, we cannot make this assumption.
Instead, a context is better if it is compatible with more tasks, because such a context can lead to more transferable encoders, and our evaluation should take this into consideration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a context and a dataset, the only things we can use are the singular values and singular functions of the context.
However, estimating the singular functions is as hard as pretraining an encoder, whereas the singular values can be efficiently estimated using the post-hoc approach in <a href="#S10" title="10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> with a small subset of samples.
Therefore, a metric will be more ideal if it only uses the singular values (the spectrum).</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show that it is possible to evaluate the usefulness of a context with only its singular values.
This might seem counter-intuitive, because suppose the encoder learns the contexture, then only the singular functions will affect the performance of the encoder.
When <math id="S14.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\cdots,\mu_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, the space spanned by <math id="S14.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1},\cdots,\phi_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is also fixed and is independent of <math id="S14.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1},\cdots,s_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1},\cdots,s_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The reason why this is possible is that the singular values and the singular functions are intrinsically connected.
Recall that <math id="S14.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})=\sum s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x)\mu_{i}(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})=\sum s_{i}^{2}\mu_{i}(x)\mu_{i}(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and that it must satisfy <math id="S14.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x^{\prime})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S14.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is quite a strict constraint.
Therefore, when <math id="S14.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\mu_{2},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, one cannot choose <math id="S14.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{2},\cdots,s_{d}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> arbitrarily.
Conversely, the singular values limit the possible choices of the singular functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose a metric for evaluating context usefulness, and the metric only depends on the singular values.
Note that there does not exist a universal metric, because whether the context is useful on a task or not depends on their compatibility, which cannot be estimated without the knowledge of the task.
However, our experiments show that the proposed metric generally works well on real datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Qualitative analysis.</h4>

<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3" title="3 Spectral Properties of a Context ‣ Chapter 1 Introduction to the Contexture Theory ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we showed that the shape of the spectrum depends on the association strength between <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, also called the association of the context.
The singular values decay slower if the association is stronger.
Here we show that the decay rate of the singular values has a great impact on the usefulness of the context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The central argument is: <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A useful context should have a moderate association</span>.
To get an intuition, consider the two extreme cases in <a href="#S3" title="3 Spectral Properties of a Context ‣ Chapter 1 Introduction to the Contexture Theory ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>:
(i) <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then there is only one positive singular value;
(ii) <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=X</annotation></semantics></math>, then all singular values are <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both contexts are clearly useless because they provide no additional information.
In what follows, we qualitatively explain why a context is not very useful if its association is too strong or too weak.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that any compatible task <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}=\sum u_{i}\mu_{i}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}=\sum u_{i}\mu_{i}\in{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in <a href="#S12.E15" title="In 12 Compatibility, Optimality of Contexture ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> needs to satisfy <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\geq 1}s_{i}^{2}u_{i}^{2}\geq(1-\epsilon)^{2}\sum_{i\geq 1}u_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\geq 1}s_{i}^{2}u_{i}^{2}\geq(1-\epsilon)^{2}\sum_{i\geq 1}u_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is easier to satisfy if <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are large.
Thus, if the association is too weak and the singular values decay too fast, then <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{\epsilon}(P^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> will be a very small set.
Consequently, very few tasks will be compatible with the context, so an encoder trained on the context will not be transferable to various tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, when the association is too strong, there are two consequences.
First, more singular functions have large singular values, and to learn all of them, one needs to use a larger <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes the sample complexity of learning the downstream linear predictor higher.
Second, the singular functions with large singular values become less smooth, so learning them requires more pretraining samples.
In <a href="#chapter5" title="Chapter 5 Statistical Learning Bounds for Representation Learning ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Chapter</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we will define the context complexity that quantitatively measures the smoothness of the top singular functions, and we will show that the context complexity is higher when the context has a stronger association.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantitative metric.</h4>

<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now propose a metric that quantitatively measures the general usefulness of a context.
The metric only depends on the singular values of the context.
The metric assumes that the pretraining method aims to learn the contexture of the context, and it does not work for an arbitrary encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our metric is defined as</p>
<table id="S14.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S14.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}=\frac{1}{1-s_{d+1}^{2}}+\beta\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{d}s_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{i=1}^{d_{0}}s_{i}^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}=\frac{1}{1-s_{d+1}^{2}}+\beta\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{d}s_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{i=1}^{d_{0}}s_{i}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a parameter, and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> we consider. Typically <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> ranges from <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8192</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8192</annotation></semantics></math>.
We choose <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=512</annotation></semantics></math> in our experiments.
<math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a proxy of the prediction error when the embedding dimension is <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as the optimal embedding dimension predicted by the metric, and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates the context when <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen optimally.
Since this metric only depends on the singular values, it can be efficiently estimated using the post-hoc approach in <a href="#S10" title="10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, with which we can estimate the spectrum using a subset of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(d_{0}\log d_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(d_{0}\log d_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This metric is derived in the following way. Let the target function be <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}=f_{0}+f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}=f_{0}+f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle f_{0},f_{1}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle f_{0},f_{1}\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is not compatible with the context, and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with the context.
Then, the prediction error can be decomposed into three components:</p>
<ol id="S14.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S14.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S14.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The approximation error of <math id="S14.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S14.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S14.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The approximation error of <math id="S14.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S14.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S14.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S14.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The estimation error</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#chapter3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4 (Proof in ). ‣ 12 Compatibility, Optimality of Contexture ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, component (i) can be bounded by <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{s_{1}^{2}-(1-\epsilon)^{2}}{s_{1}^{2}-s_{d+1}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we simplify this to the first term because <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is very close to 1 in most real cases, and the numerator is a constant.
Component (ii) is smaller if the context has a stronger association; thus, it should be negatively correlated with <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{d_{0}}s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{d_{0}}s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Component (iii) is larger if the context has a stronger association, or if <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is large.
Based on the result in <a href="#chapter5" title="Chapter 5 Statistical Learning Bounds for Representation Learning ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Chapter</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, it is positively correlated with <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{d}s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{d}s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The second term of the metric combines these two components, and it is designed to be bounded by <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Compared to previous metrics.</h4>

<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some previously proposed metrics are also based on the decay rate of the spectrum. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> proposed a metric based on the eigenvalues of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\Phi^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\Phi^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> for a particular pretrained encoder <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>; that is, they use the <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\Phi,\Phi\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}f=\lambda f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\Phi,\Phi\rangle_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}f=\lambda f</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\neq{\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\neq{\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle\Phi,\Phi\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle\Phi,\Phi\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the covariance matrix, assuming that <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is centered.
In contrast, our metric is based on the general eigenvalues satisfying <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\langle\Phi,T_{P^{+}}\Phi\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}f=s^{2}{\left\langle\Phi,\Phi\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\langle\Phi,T_{P^{+}}\Phi\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}f=s^{2}{\left\langle\Phi,\Phi\right\rangle}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}f</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\neq{\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\neq{\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as discussed in <a href="#S10" title="10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
These two sets of eigenvalues are fundamentally different.
The eigenvalues <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> we use are invariant under invertible linear transformations on <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are not.
This suggests that our metric is more desirable since invertible linear transformations on <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> do not affect the performance of the downstream linear probe.</p>
</div>
