o-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="7.2"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S14.F10.pic1.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -35.09 -25.52)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.51em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="131.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S14.F10.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">(c) Strong association</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Metric illustration on <span id="S14.F10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MNIST</span>, similar to <a href="#S14.F9" title="In Compared to previous metrics. ‣ 14 Evaluating Context Usefulness ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now we empirically examine <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> on the two datasets.
First, we apply the metric to the <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span> dataset and use KNN as the context, similar to <a href="#S11" title="11 Implications on the Scaling Law ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.
We adjust the association of the context by changing <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, we choose <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=150</annotation></semantics></math> (weak), <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=30</annotation></semantics></math> (moderate) and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=5</annotation></semantics></math> (strong).
We obtain the exact eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> using kernel PCA.
In <a href="#S14.F9" title="In Compared to previous metrics. ‣ 14 Evaluating Context Usefulness ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we plot the spectra of the three contexts in the top row.
Then, in the bottom row, we compare <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> against the prediction error of the linear probe under different <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can see that when the association is weak or moderate, <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> first decreases and then increases, which tracks the actual error.
However, when the association is too strong, <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> monotonically decreases with <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and it cannot track the actual error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we apply the metric to the <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MNIST</span> dataset.
The context is random cropping with crop ratio <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
We adjust the association of the context by changing <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, we choose <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> (weak), <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> (moderate) and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> (strong).
Since kernel PCA is not scalable to datasets as large as MNIST, we instead train a neural network.
Specifically, we train a LeNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>]</cite> using the non-contrastive learning objective formulated earlier, and the AdamW optimizer.
Then, we estimate the top eigenvalues using the post-hoc approach in <a href="#S10" title="10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
The downstream task is a binary classification task—whether the digit is greater than 4.
After pretraining, a linear probe is fit on top of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> using ridge regression.
The result is plotted in <a href="#S14.F10" title="In Compared to previous metrics. ‣ 14 Evaluating Context Usefulness ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
