.13 L 292.08 0.04 L 292.84 6.02"></path></g><g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 110.8 11.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.34em;--ltx-fo-height:0.66em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.13" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.13)" width="4.77"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S14.F11.pic1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 29.88 -31.25)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.09em;--ltx-fo-height:0.71em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.24em;font-size:10.5pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="59.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S14.F11.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">(b) <span id="S14.F11.pic1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MNIST</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>Comparison of the downstream task between <span id="S14.F11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span> and <span id="S14.F11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MNIST</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">From <a href="#S14.F10" title="In Compared to previous metrics. ‣ 14 Evaluating Context Usefulness ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we can see that when the association is not too strong, <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> first decreases and then increases, similar to <a href="#S14.F9" title="In Compared to previous metrics. ‣ 14 Evaluating Context Usefulness ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
However, on <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MNIST</span>, the downstream error monotonically decreases with <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, unlike <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span>.
This disparity is due to the difference between the two downstream tasks.
To demonstrate this, in <a href="#S14.F11" title="In Compared to previous metrics. ‣ 14 Evaluating Context Usefulness ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> we plot the cosine similarity between the target function <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and the estimated <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th eigenfunction on the two datasets.
We can see that the variance of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span> is mostly concentrated on the top-<math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions, with the first cosine similarity being almost <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast, the variance of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MNIST</span> is more scattered, and the cosine similarity is still close to <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math>-th eigenfunction.
Consequently, having a large <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span> will have a little impact on the approximation error but will increase the estimation error significantly.
On the other hand, having a larger <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MNIST</span> will decrease the approximation error more than it increases the estimation error, which is why the total error monotonically decreases with <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The takeaway from this experiment is that although in general a context with a moderate association is good, in reality it still depends on the actual downstream task.
For example, on <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">abalone</span> the weakest context actually leads to the lowest error, because the variance of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is concentrated on the top-<math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions.
On the other hand, on <span id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MNIST</span> the strongest context leads to the lowest error, because the variance of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is scattered among a lot of features, and a stronger association allows more features to be discovered.
Hence, no evaluation metric would universally work for all contexts and downstream tasks, but a metric would still be useful if it correlates well with the actual error in most scenarios, and thus can provide insights into choosing the right context and the right hyperparameters, such as the mask or crop ratio.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Empirical verification of the proposed metric.</h4>

<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although our metric is derived from the decomposition of the prediction error, it is still heuristic and is not an upper bound of the actual error. Here we show that the metric correlates well with the actual error on many real datasets.
Therefore, our metric is useful as it can help practitioners to select among various pretraining methods or choose the hyperparameters efficiently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use 28 real classification and regression datasets from OpenML that are widely used in machine learning research.
Each dataset is randomly split into a pretrain set, a labeled downstream training set, and a downstream test set by 70%-15%-15%.
For each <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the top-<math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenfunctions of <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> via kernel PCA, and then fit a linear probe on top of it using ridge regression.
Then, we select the best <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that achieves the lowest test mean squared error, which is denoted by <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The correlation between <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is reported.
The following four types of contexts are used in the experiment.</p>
<ul id="S14.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S14.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S14.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RBF kernels: <math id="S14.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,a)=\exp(-\gamma{\left\|x-a\right\|}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,a)=\exp(-\gamma{\left\|x-a\right\|}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Define <math id="S14.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(a|x)\propto k(x,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(a|x)\propto k(x,a)</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S14.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S14.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S14.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">KNN: <math id="S14.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}(a|x)=K^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}(a|x)=K^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S14.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is a KNN of <math id="S14.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, it is <math id="S14.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S14.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S14.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RBF <math id="S14.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\star" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\star</annotation></semantics></math> Masking: First randomly mask 20% of the features, and then apply RBF kernels to the other features. The <math id="S14.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of this context can be estimated as follows: first randomly draw <math id="S14.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> masks, and then compute their average <math id="S14.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{{k_{X}^{+}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S14.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S14.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S14.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">KNN <math id="S14.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\star" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\star</annotation></semantics></math> Masking: 20% random masking and then apply KNN.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each of these contexts, <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}={\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each type, 35 contexts are obtained by adjusting the <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for RBF kernels, and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> for KNN. The association between <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> for these 35 contexts are different, and the experiment makes sure that the contexts in every type range from very weak to very strong association.
“<math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\star" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\star</annotation></semantics></math> Masking” here means the convolution with masking, as mentioned in <a href="#S9" title="9 Learning from a Mixture of Contexts ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
We do not use masking alone because the dual kernel of masking is hard to estimate.</p>
</div>
