<tr id="S14.T3.2.30" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S14.T3.2.30.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" colspan="4"><span id="S14.T3.2.30.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mean</span></td>
<td id="S14.T3.2.30.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">0.431</td>
<td id="S14.T3.2.30.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">0.611</td></tr>
<tr id="S14.T3.2.31" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S14.T3.2.31.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" colspan="4"><span id="S14.T3.2.31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Median</span></td>
<td id="S14.T3.2.31.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">0.587</td>
<td id="S14.T3.2.31.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">0.659</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Correlation between <math id="S14.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and the actual error <math id="S14.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> on all 4 types of contexts.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S14.T3" title="In Empirical verification of the proposed metric. ‣ 14 Evaluating Context Usefulness ‣ Chapter 3 Intrinsic Evaluation: The Optimality of Learning the Contexture ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports the correlation between <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> over all the 140 contexts.
The most common metric is the Pearson correlation, but it can only detect linear correlations, while the correlation between <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>err</mtext><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{err}_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily linear.
Thus, we also report the distance correlation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib135" title="" class="ltx_ref">135</a>]</cite>, which is another common metric that can detect non-linear correlations but cannot tell if the correlation is positive or negative because this metric is always non-negative.</p>
</div>
