             Sample <math id="alg1.l23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{i}^{r}\sim P_{r}^{+}(\cdot|x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{r}\sim P_{r}^{+}(\cdot|x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>; Center <math id="alg1.l23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="alg1.l23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi\leftarrow\Psi-\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\Psi(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi\leftarrow\Psi-\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}\Psi(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l24" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l24.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">24:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{C}}\leftarrow{\left[\Psi(a_{1}^{r}),\cdots,\Psi(a_{m}^{r})\right]}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{C}}\leftarrow{\left[\Psi(a_{1}^{r}),\cdots,\Psi(a_{m}^{r})\right]}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times m}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l25" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l25.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">25:</span></span>
              
              
             Define <math id="alg1.l25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}=\frac{1}{m}{\left[\sum_{i=1}^{m}{\left\|\Phi(x_{i})\right\|}_{2}^{2}+{\left\|{\bm{C}}\right\|}_{F}^{2}-2{\left\langle{\bm{B}},{\bm{C}}\right\rangle}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑪</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}=\frac{1}{m}{\left[\sum_{i=1}^{m}{\left\|\Phi(x_{i})\right\|}_{2}^{2}+{\left\|{\bm{C}}\right\|}_{F}^{2}-2{\left\langle{\bm{B}},{\bm{C}}\right\rangle}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l25.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.l25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|{\bm{C}}\right\|}_{F}^{2}=\sum_{i,j}{\bm{C}}[i,j]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|{\bm{C}}\right\|}_{F}^{2}=\sum_{i,j}{\bm{C}}[i,j]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
 </span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l26" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l26.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">26:</span></span>
              
              
             Update <math id="alg1.l26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> to minimize loss <math id="alg1.l26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to <math id="alg1.l26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Standardizing kernels.</h4>

<div id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If we only have <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access to some contexts, then it is important to standardize these kernels.
We say that a <span id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p.s.d.</span> kernel <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">standardized</span>, if <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> is an eigenfunction of <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> with eigenvalue <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and all eigenvalues of <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
To see why standardizing <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1},\cdots,k_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1},\cdots,k_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is necessary, consider a case where <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has much larger eigenvalues than the other kernels; then, when mixing these contexts, context <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> could dominate over the other contexts.
We can standardize any <span id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">p.s.d.</span> kernel in three steps:</p>
<ol id="S15.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S15.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S15.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S15.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Center the kernel: <math id="S15.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,x^{\prime})\leftarrow\tilde{k}(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,x^{\prime})\leftarrow\tilde{k}(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes <math id="S15.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> an eigenfunction with eigenvalue <math id="S15.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. The centered kernel is defined as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.3</span></span><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any p.s.d. kernel <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k:{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, its <span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">centered kernel</span> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S15.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S15.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{k}(x,x^{\prime})=k(x,x^{\prime})-\int k(z,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z)-\int k(x,z^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z^{\prime})+\iint k(z,z^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z)d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z^{\prime})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mo>∬</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{k}(x,x^{\prime})=k(x,x^{\prime})-\int k(z,x^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z)-\int k(x,z^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z^{\prime})+\iint k(z,z^{\prime})d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z)d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z^{\prime}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which is a p.s.d. kernel that satisfies <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int k(x,z)d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z)=\int k(z,x)d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int k(x,z)d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z)=\int k(z,x)d{P_{\mathcal{X}}}(z)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div></li>
<li id="S15.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S15.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S15.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Divide <math id="S15.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by its largest eigenvalue, which makes all its eigenvalues at most <math id="S15.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S15.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S15.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S15.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,x^{\prime})\leftarrow k(x,x^{\prime})+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,x^{\prime})\leftarrow k(x,x^{\prime})+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes <math id="S15.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> an eigenfunction with eigenvalue <math id="S15.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The only “hyperparameter” we need to tune for a convolution is the order of the contexts, because convolution is not commutative.
In practice today, the order usually does not matter too much.
For example, it matters very little whether we first translate an image and then crop it, or we first crop it and then perform the translation.
However, as we obtain more complex contexts especially through context scaling, this order could become very important.</p>
</div>
<div id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Apart from supervised learning with feature maps discussed in <a href="#S9" title="9 Learning from a Mixture of Contexts ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, another example of convolution is supervised contrastive learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>.
Given a sample <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, supervised contrastive learning first randomly samples <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> that has the same class as <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and then augments <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}\rightarrow A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\rightarrow A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> via cropping, flipping, etc.
Supervised contrastive learning learns the convolution of the class context <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the augmentation context <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{+}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{+}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, let us discuss when we should use convolution.
In practice, convolution is used to create “harder” pretraining tasks.
For example, in self-supervised learning, multiple weak data augmentations are composed together to create a stronger augmentation.
One great example is SimCLR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, whose success is largely due to its aggressive crop ratio and color distortion, both of which make the augmentation stronger.
When an augmentation is stronger, the association between <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> becomes weaker.
Hence, convolution should be used when all contexts have strong associations, because it always weakens the association.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S16" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">16 </span>Convex Combination</h3>

<div id="S16.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A convex combination of <math id="S16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}^{+},\cdots,P_{r}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}^{+},\cdots,P_{r}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> is written as <math id="S16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}P_{1}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{r}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}P_{1}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{r}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Usually we require that <math id="S16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}=[w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}]\in\Delta^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}=[w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}]\in\Delta^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that it is a probability distribution over the <math id="S16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> contexts.
In this case, the convex combination can be understood as follows:
given an input <math id="S16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, one first samples one <math id="S16.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{j}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{j}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> from the probability distribution <math id="S16.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="S16.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> contexts, and then samples <math id="S16.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A\sim P_{j}^{+}(\cdot|X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sim P_{j}^{+}(\cdot|X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This <math id="S16.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the context variable of the convex combination.
Theoretically speaking, <math id="S16.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> can be any real values, but we assume that <math id="S16.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in this chapter unless stated otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S16.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Learning the contexture of a convex combination is simple: we only need to use a weighted sum of individual learning objectives.
Assume that we have either pair access or <math id="S16.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access to every context.
For each context <math id="S16.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we can learn its contexture using either SVME or KISE. Define its individual objective as</p>
<table id="S16.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S16.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{j}=\left\{\begin{aligned} \mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}\mathbb{E}_{A_{j}\sim P_{j}^{+}(\cdot|X)}{\left[{\left\|\Phi(X)-\Psi_{j}(A_{j})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]},\quad&amp;\text{pair access to }P_{j}^{+};\\
\mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left[{\left\|\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}-{\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}(X),T_{k_{j}}\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\rangle}\right]},\quad&amp;k\text{-access to }P^{+}_{j}.\end{aligned}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>‖</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>pair access to </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-access to </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{j}=\left\{\begin{aligned} \mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}\mathbb{E}_{A_{j}\sim P_{j}^{+}(\cdot|X)}{\left[{\left\|\Phi(X)-\Psi_{j}(A_{j})\right\|}_{2}^{2}\right]},\quad&amp;\text{pair access to }P_{j}^{+};\\
\mathbb{E}_{X\sim{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left[{\left\|\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\|}_{2}^{2}-{\left\langle\tilde{\Phi}(X),T_{k_{j}}\tilde{\Phi}(X)\right\rangle}\right]},\quad&amp;k\text{-access to }P^{+}_{j}.\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S16.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then, we can learn the contexture of their convex combination by minimizing <math id="S16.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to <math id="S16.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This approach might need more than two encoders, since it requires one <math id="S16.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for each context <math id="S16.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> with pair access.
An alternative approach is to convert every pair access to <math id="S16.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access first, but this leads to a huge overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S16.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If we have pair access to context <math id="S16.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S16.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}={k_{X}^{+}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}={k_{X}^{+}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>; if we have <math id="S16.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-access to context <math id="S16.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S16.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the kernel we have access to, and assume that it has been standardized.
The following result shows that the dual kernel of the convex combination is <math id="S16.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}k_{1}+\cdots+w_{r}k_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}k_{1}+\cdots+w_{r}k_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, the linearly combined kernel.
If every <math id="S16.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is standardized and <math id="S16.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the linearly combined kernel is also standardized.
Linearly combining multiple kernels is a classical technique in multiple kernel learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter10.A1" title="Appendix 10.A Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 10 Proofs for Chapter 4 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.A</span></a>)<span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be a minimizer of the weighted sum of objectives <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> subject to <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}[\Phi]={\bm{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts the top-<math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> eigenspace of <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}w_{j}k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}w_{j}k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S16.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Convolution and convex combination have the right distributive property.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.5</span></span><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{1}P_{1}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{r}^{+})\star P_{0}^{+}=w_{1}P_{1}^{+}\star P_{0}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{r}^{+}\star P_{0}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{1}P_{1}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{r}^{+})\star P_{0}^{+}=w_{1}P_{1}^{+}\star P_{0}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{r}^{+}\star P_{0}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S16.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S16.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
Let <math id="S16.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dual kernel of the left, and <math id="S16.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dual kernel of the right.
It suffices to show that <math id="S16.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k_{L}}=T_{k_{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k_{L}}=T_{k_{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using what was proved earlier, we have</p>
<table id="S16.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S16.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{k_{L}}=T_{k_{0}}(w_{1}T_{k_{1}}+\cdots+w_{r}T_{k_{r}})T_{k_{0}}=w_{1}T_{k_{0}}T_{k_{1}}T_{k_{0}}+\cdots+w_{r}T_{k_{0}}T_{k_{r}}T_{k_{0}}=T_{k_{R}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{k_{L}}=T_{k_{0}}(w_{1}T_{k_{1}}+\cdots+w_{r}T_{k_{r}})T_{k_{0}}=w_{1}T_{k_{0}}T_{k_{1}}T_{k_{0}}+\cdots+w_{r}T_{k_{0}}T_{k_{r}}T_{k_{0}}=T_{k_{R}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S16.p5.2" class="ltx_p">as desired.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.6</span></span><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The left distributive property does not hold, that is, <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}^{+}\star(w_{1}P_{1}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{r}^{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}^{+}\star(w_{1}P_{1}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{r}^{+})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}P_{0}^{+}\star P_{1}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{0}^{+}\star P_{r}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}P_{0}^{+}\star P_{1}^{+}+\cdots+w_{r}P_{0}^{+}\star P_{r}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> are not necessarily equal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S16.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The hyperparameters we need to tune for the convex combination are the weights <math id="S16.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, which should be chosen based on the associations of the contexts.
Usually, if a context is very weak or strong, then we would give it a small weight to limit its impact.
For example, in RLHF we have two contexts: the alignment context and the reference model context.
The alignment context has a strong association—for a given prompt, there are multiple possible valid completions <math id="S16.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, but alignment selects a small number of preferred completions from all valid ones.
Consequently, alignment reduces the conditional entropy <math id="S16.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(A|X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(A|X)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S16.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> has a stronger association with <math id="S16.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
The more selective the critic, the stronger the association.
On the other hand, the reference model context has a moderate association, provided that the reference model is well trained.
Therefore, in practice, people usually give a much larger weight to the reference model context than to the alignment context, so that the model will not be too different from the reference model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S16.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Convex combination is usually used to balance strong and weak associations.
It can also be used when all contexts have very strong associations.
In this case, we want to select the <math id="S16.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},\cdots,w_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> that weaken the association as much as possible.
We show that this can be achieved by playing a zero-sum game between a <math id="S16.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>-player who learns the encoders, and a <math id="S16.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>-player who picks <math id="S16.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize the loss of the <math id="S16.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>-player.
The game has the following minimax form.</p>
<table id="S16.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S16.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d};\;\Psi_{j}:{\mathcal{A}}_{j}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\;\max_{{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r+1}}\;{\mathcal{L}}(\Phi,\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r};{\bm{w}}):=\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}\quad\text{s.t. }\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left[\Phi\right]}={\bm{I}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒜</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ℝ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>s.t. </mtext><msub><mi>Cov</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d};\;\Psi_{j}:{\mathcal{A}}_{j}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\textrm{minimize}}\;\;\max_{{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r+1}}\;{\mathcal{L}}(\Phi,\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r};{\bm{w}}):=\sum\nolimits_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}\quad\text{s.t. }\mathrm{Cov}_{{P_{\mathcal{X}}}}{\left[\Phi\right]}={\bm{I}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S16.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S16.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is allowed to be randomized, that is the <math id="S16.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>-player can use a mixed strategy.
Obviously, the <math id="S16.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>-player has an optimal pure strategy, so <math id="S16.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> need not to be randomized.
Let us analyze the Nash equilibrium of this game.
First, notice that the <math id="S16.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>-player only needs to pick <math id="S16.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Once <math id="S16.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is picked, the optimal <math id="S16.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>’s can be determined as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.7</span></span><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose context <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> has pair access. Then, when <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, the optimal <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}^{*}=T_{P_{j}^{+}}^{*}\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}^{*}=T_{P_{j}^{+}}^{*}\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S16.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S16.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
This is the same as the proof of <a href="#chapter2.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 2.11 (Proof in ). ‣ 7 General Objectives: SVME and KISE ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S16.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p9.1" class="ltx_p">When both players play optimally and the game reaches Nash equilibrium,
the value of <math id="S16.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\Phi}[{\mathcal{L}}(\Phi,\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r};{\bm{w}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\Phi}[{\mathcal{L}}(\Phi,\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r};{\bm{w}})]</annotation></semantics></math> is called the value of this game, denoted by <math id="S16.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. The expectation is taken over the randomness of <math id="S16.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following result gives the optimal strategy of the <math id="S16.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>-player, and the formula for <math id="S16.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.8</span></span><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\lambda_{0}({\bm{w}})\geq\lambda_{1}({\bm{w}})\geq\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\lambda_{0}({\bm{w}})\geq\lambda_{1}({\bm{w}})\geq\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> be the eigenvalues of <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{w_{1}k_{1}+\cdots+w_{r}k_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{w_{1}k_{1}+\cdots+w_{r}k_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the optimal strategy of the <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>-player.
Then, the game value of <a href="#S16.E19" title="In 16 Convex Combination ‣ Chapter 4 Mixing Multiple Contexts ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> is</span></p>
<table id="S16.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S16.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}^{*}=d-\sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}({\bm{w}}^{*}),\quad\text{ and }{\bm{w}}^{*}\text{ minimizes }\sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}({\bm{w}})\text{ over all }{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> minimizes </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> over all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒘</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}^{*}=d-\sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}({\bm{w}}^{*}),\quad\text{ and }{\bm{w}}^{*}\text{ minimizes }\sum\nolimits_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}({\bm{w}})\text{ over all }{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S16.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S16.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
For a fixed <math id="S16.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>, when <math id="S16.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> learns the contexture of <math id="S16.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}w_{j}k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}w_{j}k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, the loss is <math id="S16.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}=d-\sum_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}({\bm{w}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}=d-\sum_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}({\bm{w}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the optimal <math id="S16.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes <math id="S16.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> must minimize <math id="S16.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}({\bm{w}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{d}\lambda_{i}({\bm{w}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Convex combination: Solving the minimax game</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Step size <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Initialize encoder <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\mathcal{X}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{j}:{\mathcal{A}}_{j}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{j}:{\mathcal{A}}_{j}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> if needed; <math id="alg2.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}\leftarrow[1/r,\cdots,1/r]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}\leftarrow[1/r,\cdots,1/r]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> training step <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\cdots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\cdots,T</annotation></semantics></math>  <span id="alg2.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
             Fix <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and find the optimal <math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi,\Psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi,\Psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to <a href="#S16.E19" title="In 16 Convex Combination ‣ Chapter 4 Mixing Multiple Contexts ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>. Denote the optimal <math id="alg2.l4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="alg2.l4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="alg2.l4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
             Compute <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{1},\cdots,{\mathcal{R}}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{1},\cdots,{\mathcal{R}}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>; Update <math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}\leftarrow w_{j}\cdot\exp(\eta{\mathcal{R}}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}\leftarrow w_{j}\cdot\exp(\eta{\mathcal{R}}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, then normalize <math id="alg2.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}\leftarrow\frac{w_{j}}{\sum_{i=1}^{r}w_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}\leftarrow\frac{w_{j}}{\sum_{i=1}^{r}w_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            The <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>-player picks the uniform distribution over <math id="alg2.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{1},\Phi^{2},\cdots,\Phi^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{1},\Phi^{2},\cdots,\Phi^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> (a randomized <math id="alg2.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S16.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p11.1" class="ltx_p">To solve this game, we can use an algorithm similar to the Hedge algorithm in online learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.
The algorithm is listed in <a href="#alg2" title="In 16 Convex Combination ‣ Chapter 4 Mixing Multiple Contexts ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
A standard result in online learning shows that this algorithm can find the value of this game.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.9</span></span><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter10.A2" title="Appendix 10.B Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 10 Proofs for Chapter 4 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.B</span></a>)<span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{j}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{j}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the loss <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in step <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose there exists a constant <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{j}^{t}\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{j}^{t}\leq C</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,j</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{w}})=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}{\left(\sum_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}^{t}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{w}})=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}{\left(\sum_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}^{t}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\frac{\sqrt{\log r}}{C\sqrt{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msqrt><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\frac{\sqrt{\log r}}{C\sqrt{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;\log r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;\log r</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S16.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S16.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r+1}}{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{w}})\leq\max_{{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r+1}}\;\min_{\Phi,\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r}}\;\sum_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}+\frac{2C\sqrt{\log r}}{\sqrt{T}}={\mathcal{L}}^{*}+\frac{2C\sqrt{\log r}}{\sqrt{T}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r+1}}{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{w}})\leq\max_{{\bm{w}}\in\Delta^{r+1}}\;\min_{\Phi,\Psi_{1},\cdots,\Psi_{r}}\;\sum_{j=1}^{r}w_{j}{\mathcal{R}}_{j}+\frac{2C\sqrt{\log r}}{\sqrt{T}}={\mathcal{L}}^{*}+\frac{2C\sqrt{\log r}}{\sqrt{T}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which implies that <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{w}})\rightarrow{\mathcal{L}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}({\bm{w}})\rightarrow{\mathcal{L}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="chapter4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S16.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S16.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Convex combination cannot be used when all contexts have weak associations, because the decay rate of its eigenfunctions is upper bounded by that of the strongest context. That is, convex combination cannot make the association stronger.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S17" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">17 </span>Concatenation</h3>

<div id="S17.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S17.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Concatenation is easy to implement: For each context <math id="S17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, one trains an encoder <math id="S17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then concatenates them into a single encoder by <math id="S17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x)=[\Phi_{1}(x),\cdots,\Phi_{r}(x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x)=[\Phi_{1}(x),\cdots,\Phi_{r}(x)]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Concatenation is used when all contexts have weak associations.
We cannot learn rich features from a context with a weak association, but if we have several such contexts and concatenate them, then the features will be richer.
Since concatenation could lead to redundant features that increase the sample complexity, it should not be used for contexts with very strong associations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S17.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S17.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further elaborate on concatenation, let us suppose <math id="S17.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set, and <math id="S17.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{X}}|=N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{X}}|=N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the dual kernel of each context <math id="S17.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S17.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times N</annotation></semantics></math> matrix denoted by <math id="S17.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{K}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{K}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The dual kernel of the concatenation can be understood as <math id="S17.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left(\begin{matrix}{\bm{K}}_{1}&amp;&amp;&amp;0\\
&{\bm{K}}_{2}&amp;&amp;\\
&amp;&amp;\ddots&amp;\\
0&amp;&amp;&{\bm{K}}_{r}\end{matrix}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left(\begin{matrix}{\bm{K}}_{1}&amp;&amp;&amp;0\\
&amp;{\bm{K}}_{2}&amp;&amp;\\
&amp;&amp;\ddots&amp;\\
0&amp;&amp;&amp;{\bm{K}}_{r}\end{matrix}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose eigenvalues are the union of the eigenvalues of every individual <math id="S17.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{K}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑲</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{K}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, the eigenvalues of the concatenation decay more slowly than those of any individual context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S17.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S17.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The hyperparameters of concatenation are the dimensions of <math id="S17.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{1},\cdots,\Phi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{1},\cdots,\Phi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S17.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},\cdots,d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},\cdots,d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One good way to select them is the following: first, estimate the singular values <math id="S17.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}^{(j)},s_{2}^{(j)},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}^{(j)},s_{2}^{(j)},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> of each context <math id="S17.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> using the post-hoc approach in <a href="#S10" title="10 Extracting Exact Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues ‣ Chapter 2 Learning the Contexture with Variational Objectives ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>;
then, select <math id="S17.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},\cdots,d_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},\cdots,d_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S17.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{d_{1}+1}^{(1)},s_{d_{2}+1}^{(2)},\cdots,s_{d_{r}+1}^{(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{d_{1}+1}^{(1)},s_{d_{2}+1}^{(2)},\cdots,s_{d_{r}+1}^{(r)}</annotation></semantics></math> are all close and small.</p>
</div>
<div id="S17.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S17.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S17.T4" title="In 17 Concatenation ‣ Chapter 4 Mixing Multiple Contexts ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> summarizes when to use each of the three base operations.
We can see that these three operations cover all possible scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S17.T4" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S17.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S17.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S17.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Base operation</th>
<td id="S17.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Concatenation</td>
<td id="S17.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Convolution</td>
<td id="S17.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Convex combination</td></tr>
<tr id="S17.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S17.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">When to use?</th>
<td id="S17.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">All weak associations</td>
<td id="S17.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">All strong associations</td>
<td id="S17.T4.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Mixed weak/strong</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>When to use each base operation of mixing contexts.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S18" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">18 </span>Application to Tabular Data</h3>

<div id="S18.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The methods of mixing contexts provide us with an opportunity to create better contexts almost effortlessly.
Here we test these methods on real-world tabular datasets. We focus on tabular data for two reasons:</p>
<ol id="S18.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S18.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S18.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S18.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is important: Tabular data is the most common type of data in industry.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S18.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S18.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S18.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is challenging: So far deep learning has not been as successful on tabular data as it is on other modalities. In particular, XGBoost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> has long been the state of the art on tabular data.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S18.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S18.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are several complications of tabular data that make it more difficult for deep learning than image and text:</p>
<ol id="S18.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S18.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S18.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S18.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Heterogeneous features, such as categorical and numerical columns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S18.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S18.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S18.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Features have different meanings, unlike <span id="S18.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span> images where all features are pixels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S18.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S18.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S18.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Much lower signal-to-noise ratio than other modalities, and missing values (NaNs).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use 118 datasets and run a grid search on tens of thousands of hyperparameter combinations for each method and dataset.
Using a large number of datasets and hyperparameters ensures that the bias of dataset and hyperparameter selection is reduced as much as possible.
We focus on prediction tasks, and use 98 classification datasets and 20 regression datasets (labeled <span id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">reg-20</span>).
The 98 classification datasets further consist of 56 smaller ones (labeled <span id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cls-56</span>) with fewer than 1500 samples, and 42 larger ones (labeled <span id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cls-42</span>).
All datasets are real-world datasets from OpenML <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib146" title="" class="ltx_ref">146</a>]</cite>, and they cover a wide range of domains.
We use 10 train-val-test splits (which we call 10 folds) for each dataset and report the standard deviation of performance.
For evaluation, our metrics are the accuracy for classification, and the <math id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-score for regression.
We consider two performances close if their difference is less than one standard deviation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by evaluating the baseline methods and two recent methods that are widely compared to in the literature—FT-Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> and TabPFN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>.
<a href="#S18.T5" title="In Baselines. ‣ 18 Application to Tabular Data ‣ Chapter 4 Mixing Multiple Contexts ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the average performances and rankings of eight baseline methods.
For the rankings, we rank the methods from <math id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S18.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> on each dataset (ties get the same ranking) and take the average for every method.
From the table we can see that:</p>
<ul id="S18.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S18.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S18.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S18.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to the prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>]</cite>, most methods (except CatBoost and TabPFN) get much higher performance in our experiments. In their paper, ResNet was reported to be much better than MLP, which is not very reasonable. On the other hand, in our experiments, MLP and ResNet have almost the same performance. This shows that careful hyperparameter tuning gives us more accurate baselines.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S18.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S18.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S18.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To our surprise, MLP and ResNet perform the best on the 56 small classification data sets, though conventional wisdom suggests that deep learning is bad on small data sets. However, XGBoost has a much higher performance than MLP on the 42 larger classification data sets and the regression data sets. Based on this observation, we use MLP and XGBoost as our main baselines.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S18.T5" class="ltx_table">
