content"><math id="S19.F15.pic1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><clipPath id="pgfcp41"><path d="M -12.71 0 L 139.84 0 L 139.84 57.08 L -12.71 57.08 Z"></path></clipPath><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp41)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B3B3B3;" fill="#B3B3B3"><path d="M 0 0 h 4.24 v 2.74 h -4.24 Z M 4.24 0 h 4.24 v 2.74 h -4.24 Z M 8.47 0 h 4.24 v 0.91 h -4.24 Z M 12.71 0 h 4.24 v 2.28 h -4.24 Z M 16.95 0 h 4.24 v 3.2 h -4.24 Z M 21.19 0 h 4.24 v 3.2 h -4.24 Z M 25.42 0 h 4.24 v 6.85 h -4.24 Z M 29.66 0 h 4.24 v 7.31 h -4.24 Z M 33.9 0 h 4.24 v 6.39 h -4.24 Z M 38.14 0 h 4.24 v 4.57 h -4.24 Z M 42.37 0 h 4.24 v 9.13 h -4.24 Z M 46.61 0 h 4.24 v 7.31 h -4.24 Z M 50.85 0 h 4.24 v 8.22 h -4.24 Z M 55.09 0 h 4.24 v 8.22 h -4.24 Z M 59.32 0 h 4.24 v 16.44 h -4.24 Z M 63.56 0 h 4.24 v 18.26 h -4.24 Z M 67.8 0 h 4.24 v 31.51 h -4.24 Z M 72.04 0 h 4.24 v 42.01 h -4.24 Z M 76.27 0 h 4.24 v 38.36 h -4.24 Z M 80.51 0 h 4.24 v 52.97 h -4.24 Z M 84.75 0 h 4.24 v 47.03 h -4.24 Z M 88.99 0 h 4.24 v 40.64 h -4.24 Z M 93.22 0 h 4.24 v 35.62 h -4.24 Z M 97.46 0 h 4.24 v 25.11 h -4.24 Z M 101.7 0 h 4.24 v 17.35 h -4.24 Z M 105.94 0 h 4.24 v 9.59 h -4.24 Z M 110.17 0 h 4.24 v 4.11 h -4.24 Z M 114.41 0 h 4.24 v 2.74 h -4.24 Z M 118.65 0 h 4.24 v 1.37 h -4.24 Z M 122.89 0 h 4.24 v 0.46 h -4.24 Z"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 114.37 0 L 114.37 57.08"></path></g><g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 36.46 70.27)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.92em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="54.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S19.F15.pic1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.40</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15: </span>Histograms of <math id="S19.F15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for random masking on <span id="S19.F15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">wikipedia-simple</span> with mask ratio <math id="S19.F15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The dashed vertical line in each plot indicates the <math id="S19.F15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>99</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> percentile.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A natural idea is to estimate <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)</annotation></semantics></math> for a random subset of samples, and output their maximum as an estimate of <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, this approach has two issues.
First, <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x}{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x}{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)</annotation></semantics></math> is statistically impossible to estimate from a subset of data without any extra assumptions on the distribution of <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Second, almost all real datasets contain outliers, which are very different from most samples.
These outliers have very large <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)</annotation></semantics></math>, but given that these outliers have little impact on the actual pretraining, we do not want to take these large <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)</annotation></semantics></math> into account.
To fix these two issues, we can use the <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>99</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> percentile of <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
First, the percentile can be estimated with a finite confidence interval via sampling regardless of the distribution of <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, Section 5.2]</cite>.
Second, we get rid of the outliers if they are fewer than 1 percent.
<a href="#S19.F15" title="In Real language models. ‣ 19 Context Complexity ‣ Chapter 5 Statistical Learning Bounds for Representation Learning ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> plots the histograms of <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for random masking on <span id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">wikipedia-simple</span>.
The dashed line in each plot indicates the <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>99</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> percentile.
We can see from the plots that the <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>99</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> percentile is a good choice, as it picks out the outliers where <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{k_{X}^{+}}(x,x)</annotation></semantics></math> is too large.</p>
</div>
<div id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S19.F16" title="In Real language models. ‣ 19 Context Complexity ‣ Chapter 5 Statistical Learning Bounds for Representation Learning ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> (left) plots the <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>99</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> percentile of <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\kappa^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\kappa^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of four contexts: random masking, random masking with flipping, block masking, and block masking with flipping.
Masking randomly masks <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> of the tokens.
Masking with flipping masks <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha/2</annotation></semantics></math> of the tokens and replaces another <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha/2</annotation></semantics></math> of the tokens with random tokens.
Note that this replace rate is higher than the common 80-10-10 strategy in NLP, because we want to magnify the effect of flipping.
From the plot, we can see that the complexity drops as <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> increases as expected.
One observation is that the “Random + Flip” curve intersects with “Block” and “Block + Flip”,
suggesting that block masking has a stronger effect when <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is small, whereas flipping has a stronger effect when <math id="S19.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is large.</p>
</div>
