" width="117.04"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S25.F20.pic1.25" class="ltx_text">(d) Training loss (<math id="S25.F20.pic1.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.8 0.0 0.0 0.8 307.35 -61.82)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:10.36em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="143.41"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S25.F20.pic1.26" class="ltx_text">(e) Weight difference (<math id="S25.F20.pic1.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=10</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.8 0.0 0.0 0.8 318.7 -241.7)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.21em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="113.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S25.F20.pic1.27" class="ltx_text">(f) Training loss (<math id="S25.F20.pic1.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=10</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 20: </span>Experiment results of ERM, importance weighting (IW) and group DRO (GDRO) with the squared loss and <math id="S25.F20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization on six MNIST images with a linear model. <math id="S25.F20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularization coefficient. All norms are <math id="S25.F20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norms.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To conclude, we gain the following insight from the above analysis:
Without a very large regularization, early stopping or altering the training set, GRW and ERM will produce very similar models, so GRW cannot be better than ERM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Wide neural networks, regression tasks.</h4>

<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With this insight, we now study neural networks.
In particular, we focus on <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sufficiently wide fully-connected neural networks</span> within the neural tangent kernel (NTK) regime <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>.
The neural network is defined as</p>
<table id="S25.Ex82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{h}}^{l+1}=\frac{{\bm{W}}^{l}}{\sqrt{d_{l}}}{\bm{x}}^{l}+\beta{\bm{b}}^{l}\;;\qquad{\bm{x}}^{0}=x,\;{\bm{x}}^{l+1}=\sigma({\bm{h}}^{l+1}).\quad(l=0,\cdots,L)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{h}}^{l+1}=\frac{{\bm{W}}^{l}}{\sqrt{d_{l}}}{\bm{x}}^{l}+\beta{\bm{b}}^{l}\;;\qquad{\bm{x}}^{0}=x,\;{\bm{x}}^{l+1}=\sigma({\bm{h}}^{l+1}).\quad(l=0,\cdots,L)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-linear activation function, <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{l+1}\times d_{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{l+1}\times d_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>L</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times d_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=d_{{\mathcal{X}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=d_{{\mathcal{X}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The parameter vector <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}^{0},\cdots,{\bm{W}}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑾</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}^{0},\cdots,{\bm{W}}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}^{0},\cdots,{\bm{b}}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}^{0},\cdots,{\bm{b}}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is the concatenation of all flattened weights and biases). The final output is <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)={\bm{h}}^{L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)={\bm{h}}^{L+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A wide neural network has large <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1},\cdots,d_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1},\cdots,d_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the neural network is initialized as</p>
<table id="S25.Ex83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\begin{aligned} &{\bm{W}}_{i,j}^{l(0)}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)\\
&{\bm{b}}_{j}^{l(0)}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)\end{aligned}\right.\quad(l=0,\cdots,L-1)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\left\{\begin{aligned} &{\bm{W}}_{i,j}^{L(0)}=0\\
&{\bm{b}}_{j}^{L(0)}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)\end{aligned}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>and</mtext><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;{\bm{W}}_{i,j}^{l(0)}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)\\
&amp;{\bm{b}}_{j}^{l(0)}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)\end{aligned}\right.\quad(l=0,\cdots,L-1)\qquad\text{and}\qquad\left\{\begin{aligned} &amp;{\bm{W}}_{i,j}^{L(0)}=0\\
&amp;{\bm{b}}_{j}^{L(0)}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,1)\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we assume that <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable everywhere; and both <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and its first-order derivative <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> are Lipschitz, meaning that there exists a constant <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(x_{1})-f(x_{2})|\leq L{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(x_{1})-f(x_{2})|\leq L{\left\|x_{1}-x_{2}\right\|}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the rest of this chapter, we will use <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">wide NN</span> to refer to a neural network that satisfies all the above conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">neural tangent kernel</span> (NTK) is defined as <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{(0)}(x,x^{\prime})=\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)^{\top}\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{(0)}(x,x^{\prime})=\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)^{\top}\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our result is based on the following NTK theorem proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.5</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz and <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{l}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{l}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\cdots,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\cdots,L</annotation></semantics></math> sequentially, then <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{(0)}(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{(0)}(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> converges in probability to a non-degenerate deterministic limiting kernel <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here “non-degenerate” means that <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(x,x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(x,x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and is not a constant.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">kernel Gram matrix</span> <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta=\Theta({\bm{X}},{\bm{X}})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta=\Theta({\bm{X}},{\bm{X}})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive semi-definite symmetric matrix. Denote its largest and smallest eigenvalues by <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> is non-degenerate, so we can assume that <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\min}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\min}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (which is almost surely true when <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{L}\gg n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{L}\gg n</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.6</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter12.A2" title="Appendix 12.B Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 12 Proofs for Chapter 6 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.B</span></a>)<span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> be a wide NN trained by any GRW method satisfying <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 6.3. ‣ Insights from linear models. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> with the squared loss.
Let <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}_{\textnormal{ERM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>ERM</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}_{\textnormal{ERM}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the same model trained by ERM from the same initial point.
Suppose <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=\cdots=d_{L}=\tilde{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=\cdots=d_{L}=\tilde{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{1}),\cdots,\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{1}),\cdots,\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent, and <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\min}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\min}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, with a sufficiently small <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}(\lambda)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}(\lambda)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that when <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{D}(\lambda)\leq\tilde{d}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{D}(\lambda)\leq\tilde{d}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> over random initialization, there is</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\limsup_{t\rightarrow\infty}\left|f^{(t)}(x)-f^{(t)}_{\textnormal{ERM}}(x)\right|=O(\tilde{d}^{-1/4})\rightarrow 0\qquad\text{for all }x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\text{ such that }{\left\|x\right\|}_{2}\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>ERM</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> such that </mtext><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup_{t\rightarrow\infty}\left|f^{(t)}(x)-f^{(t)}_{\textnormal{ERM}}(x)\right|=O(\tilde{d}^{-1/4})\rightarrow 0\qquad\text{for all }x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\text{ such that }{\left\|x\right\|}_{2}\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This theorem says that on any test point <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in the unit ball, the GRW model and the ERM model produce almost the same output.
Thus, the two models must have similar OOD generalization performance.
Note that for simplicity, we only prove for <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=\cdots=d_{L}=\tilde{d}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=\cdots=d_{L}=\tilde{d}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, but the result can be very easily extended to the case where <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{l}/d_{1}\rightarrow\alpha_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{l}/d_{1}\rightarrow\alpha_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=2,\cdots,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=2,\cdots,L</annotation></semantics></math> for some constants <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2},\cdots,\alpha_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2},\cdots,\alpha_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The key of proving this theorem is to consider the following <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linearized neural network</span>:</p>
<table id="S25.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(t)}_{\textnormal{lin}}(x)=f^{(0)}(x)+{\left\langle\theta^{(t)}-\theta^{(0)},\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)\right\rangle}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>lin</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(t)}_{\textnormal{lin}}(x)=f^{(0)}(x)+{\left\langle\theta^{(t)}-\theta^{(0)},\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)\right\rangle},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">which is a linear model <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w.r.t.</span> <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{1}),\cdots,\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{1}),\cdots,\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent (which is almost surely true when the model is overparameterized so that <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> has a very high dimension), then our previous insight tells us that the linearized network will converge to the unique interpolator.
It then suffices to show that the wide NN can be approximated by its linearized version uniformly throughout training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us study the effect of <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization, with which the GRW learning objective becomes</p>
<table id="S25.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathcal{R}}}_{{\bm{q}}^{(t)}}^{\mu}(f)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}^{(t)}\ell(f(x_{i}),y_{i})+\frac{\mu}{2}{\left\|\theta-\theta^{(0)}\right\|}_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>𝒒</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathcal{R}}}_{{\bm{q}}^{(t)}}^{\mu}(f)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}^{(t)}\ell(f(x_{i}),y_{i})+\frac{\mu}{2}{\left\|\theta-\theta^{(0)}\right\|}_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Adding regularization does make a difference to the model regardless of how big <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is.
However, to make it possible for GRW to improve over ERM,
the regularization must be large enough to <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">significantly lower the training performance</span>. Otherwise, the final model would still be too close to the unregularized ERM model.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.7</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter12.A3" title="Appendix 12.C Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 12 Proofs for Chapter 6 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.C</span></a>)<span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>reg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> be a regularized wide NN trained by any GRW algorithm satisfying <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 6.3. ‣ Insights from linear models. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> with the squared loss. Suppose there exists <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)\right\|}_{2}\leq M_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)\right\|}_{2}\leq M_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|x\right\|}_{2}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|x\right\|}_{2}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\min}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\min}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}=\cdots=d_{L}=\tilde{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}=\cdots=d_{L}=\tilde{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{1}),\cdots,\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{1}),\cdots,\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent, and the learning rate is sufficiently small.
If the empirical training risk of <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>reg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\limsup_{t\rightarrow\infty}\hat{{\mathcal{R}}}(f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)})&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>reg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup_{t\rightarrow\infty}\hat{{\mathcal{R}}}(f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)})&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then as <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{d}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{d}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability close to <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> over random initialization there is</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\limsup_{t\rightarrow\infty}\left|f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)}(x)-f_{\textnormal{ERM}}^{(t)}(x)\right|=O(\tilde{d}^{-1/4}+\sqrt{\epsilon})\rightarrow O(\sqrt{\epsilon})\quad\text{for all }x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\text{ such that }{\left\|x\right\|}_{2}\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>reg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>ERM</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>ϵ</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>ϵ</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> such that </mtext><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup_{t\rightarrow\infty}\left|f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)}(x)-f_{\textnormal{ERM}}^{(t)}(x)\right|=O(\tilde{d}^{-1/4}+\sqrt{\epsilon})\rightarrow O(\sqrt{\epsilon})\quad\text{for all }x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\text{ such that }{\left\|x\right\|}_{2}\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Linear models, classification tasks.</h4>

<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far we have been discussing regression tasks.
We now move on to binary classification, where the label space is <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}=\{+1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}=\{+1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the loss is the logistic loss <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\hat{y},y)=\log(1+\exp(-\hat{y}y))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\hat{y},y)=\log(1+\exp(-\hat{y}y))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The big difference here is that <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the logistic loss does not have finite minimizers</span>. The logistic loss converging to zero means that the model weight “explodes” to infinity instead of converging to a finite point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Again consider the linear model <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)={\left\langle\theta,x\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)={\left\langle\theta,x\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Prior work has shown a couple of negative results under this setting.
For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> empirically observed that importance weighting does not improve over ERM for linear models, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib162" title="" class="ltx_ref">162</a>]</cite> proved that for importance weighting, as <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\theta^{(t)}\|_{2}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\theta^{(t)}\|_{2}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{(t)}/\|\theta^{(t)}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{(t)}/\|\theta^{(t)}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to a unit vector that does not depend on the sample weights, so it does not improve over ERM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We extend these results to GRW. First, we show that when the training error goes to zero, <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> will converge to the <span id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">max-margin classifier</span> defined as</p>
<table id="S25.Ex86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}=\argmax_{\theta:{\left\|\theta\right\|}_{2}=1}{\left\{\min_{i=1,\cdots,n}y_{i}\cdot{\left\langle\theta,x_{i}\right\rangle}\right\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>MM</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>{</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}=\argmax_{\theta:{\left\|\theta\right\|}_{2}=1}{\left\{\min_{i=1,\cdots,n}y_{i}\cdot{\left\langle\theta,x_{i}\right\rangle}\right\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.8</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter12.A4" title="Appendix 12.D Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 12 Proofs for Chapter 6 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.D</span></a>)<span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent. Suppose we use GRW such that for all <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\liminf_{t\rightarrow\infty}q_{i}^{(t)}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim inf</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\liminf_{t\rightarrow\infty}q_{i}^{(t)}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, if the empirical risk <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathcal{R}}}(f^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathcal{R}}}(f^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math> converges to zero and <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{(t)}/{\left\|\theta^{(t)}\right\|}_{2}\rightarrow{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{(t)}/{\left\|\theta^{(t)}\right\|}_{2}\rightarrow{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some unit vector <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}=\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>MM</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}=\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This result is an extension of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib133" title="" class="ltx_ref">133</a>]</cite>. It implies that all GRW methods including ERM, if converge, must converge to the same point <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>MM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}</annotation></semantics></math> that does not depend on the sample weights <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, we show that any GRW satisfying <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 6.3. ‣ Insights from linear models. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> does converge.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.9</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A first-order differentiable function <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-smooth</span> for <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex87" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y)\leq f(x)+\langle\nabla f(x),y-x\rangle+\frac{L}{2}\left\|y-x\right\|_{2}^{2}\qquad\text{for all }x,y\in{\mathcal{D}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y)\leq f(x)+\langle\nabla f(x),y-x\rangle+\frac{L}{2}\left\|y-x\right\|_{2}^{2}\qquad\text{for all }x,y\in{\mathcal{D}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An equivalent definition is that <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth if it satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S25.Ex88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\nabla f(x)-\nabla f(y)\right\|}_{2}\leq L{\left\|x-y\right\|}_{2}\qquad\text{for all }x,y\in{\mathcal{D}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\nabla f(x)-\nabla f(y)\right\|}_{2}\leq L{\left\|x-y\right\|}_{2}\qquad\text{for all }x,y\in{\mathcal{D}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.10</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter12.A5" title="Appendix 12.E Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 12 Proofs for Chapter 6 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.E</span></a>)<span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose the loss function <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\hat{y},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\hat{y},y)</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth in <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and strictly monotonically decreasing to zero as <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\hat{y}\rightarrow+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\hat{y}\rightarrow+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the linear model <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)={\left\langle\theta,x\right\rangle}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)={\left\langle\theta,x\right\rangle}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\cdots,x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent.
For any GRW that satisfies <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 6.3. ‣ Insights from linear models. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> with <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}\ell(\langle\theta,x_{i}\rangle,y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}\ell(\langle\theta,x_{i}\rangle,y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for a sufficiently small learning rate <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S25.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S25.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S25.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta^{(t)})\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta^{(t)})\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as </span><math id="S25.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S25.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S25.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\theta^{(t)}\right\|_{2}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\theta^{(t)}\right\|_{2}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as </span><math id="S25.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S25.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S25.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S25.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S25.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{R}=\argmin_{\theta}\{F(\theta):\left\|\theta\right\|_{2}\leq R\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{R}=\argmin_{\theta}\{F(\theta):\left\|\theta\right\|_{2}\leq R\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. </span><math id="S25.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is unique for any </span><math id="S25.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S25.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\|\theta\|_{2}\leq R}F(\theta)&lt;\min_{i}q_{i}\ell(0,y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\|\theta\|_{2}\leq R}F(\theta)&lt;\min_{i}q_{i}\ell(0,y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. And if </span><math id="S25.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{R\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\theta_{R}}{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{R\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\theta_{R}}{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> exists, then </span><math id="S25.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\theta^{(t)}}{\left\|\theta^{(t)}\right\|_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\theta^{(t)}}{\left\|\theta^{(t)}\right\|_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S25.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> also exists and they are equal.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This result is an extension of Theorem 1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>.
We show that the logistic loss satisfies the conditions of the above theorem and <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{R\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\theta_{R}}{R}=\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>MM</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{R\rightarrow\infty}\frac{\theta_{R}}{R}=\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#chapter12.A6" title="Appendix 12.F Analysis of the Logistic Loss ‣ Chapter 12 Proofs for Chapter 6 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.F</span></a>.
Thus, <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 6.8 (Proof in ). ‣ Linear models, classification tasks. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.8</span></a> and <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 6.10 (Proof in ). ‣ Linear models, classification tasks. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a> imply that for a linear model, all GRW methods satisfying <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 6.3. ‣ Insights from linear models. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> (including ERM) will converge to the same point.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Wide neural networks, classification tasks.</h4>

<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now study wide NNs with regularization.
But before that, we have to point out that it is impossible to extend <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 6.10 (Proof in ). ‣ Linear models, classification tasks. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a> to a wide NN without regularization.
This is because for a neural network, if <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\theta^{(t)}\right\|}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\theta^{(t)}\right\|}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> goes to infinity, then <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\nabla_{\theta}f\right\|}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\nabla_{\theta}f\right\|}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> will also go to infinity, unlike a linear model where this gradient is a constant.
Consequently, the gap between the neural networks and its linearized counterpart will “explode” under gradient descent, so we cannot prove that the wide NN can always be approximated by its linearized version similar to the previous section.
However, with regularization, an approximation theorem can be proved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider minimizing the regularized risk <a href="#S25.E30" title="In Wide neural networks, regression tasks. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a> with a wide NN, and <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is the logistic loss.
Define the max-margin linearized NN as</p>
<table id="S25.Ex89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\textnormal{MM}}(x)={\left\langle\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}},\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)\right\rangle}\quad\text{where}\quad\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}=\argmax_{\|\theta\|_{2}=1}{\left\{\min_{i=1,\cdots,n}y_{i}\cdot{\left\langle\theta,\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{i})\right\rangle}\right\}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>MM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>MM</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>MM</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\textnormal{MM}}(x)={\left\langle\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}},\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)\right\rangle}\quad\text{where}\quad\hat{\theta}_{\textnormal{MM}}=\argmax_{\|\theta\|_{2}=1}{\left\{\min_{i=1,\cdots,n}y_{i}\cdot{\left\langle\theta,\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x_{i})\right\rangle}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\textnormal{MM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>MM</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\textnormal{MM}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can again show that regularization only works when it is large enough to sufficiently downgrade the training performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.11</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter12.A7" title="Appendix 12.G Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 12 Proofs for Chapter 6 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.G</span></a>)<span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\left\|\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)\right\|}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\left\|\nabla_{\theta}f^{(0)}(x)\right\|}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded.
Under the same conditions of <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 6.7 (Proof in ). ‣ Wide neural networks, regression tasks. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>, when <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is the logistic loss, for any <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a constant <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\delta)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\delta)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that with probability at least <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds as <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{d}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{d}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any </span><math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,\frac{1}{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,\frac{1}{4})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, if the training error has </span><math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\limsup_{t\rightarrow\infty}\hat{{\mathcal{R}}}_{{\bm{q}}^{(t)}}^{\mu}(f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)})&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>𝒒</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>reg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup_{t\rightarrow\infty}\hat{{\mathcal{R}}}_{{\bm{q}}^{(t)}}^{\mu}(f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)})&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then for any </span><math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|f_{\textnormal{MM}}(x)|&gt;C(\delta)\cdot(-\log 2\epsilon)^{-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>MM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">&gt;</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f_{\textnormal{MM}}(x)|&gt;C(\delta)\cdot(-\log 2\epsilon)^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext>reg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\textnormal{reg}}^{(t)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has the same sign as </span><math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\textnormal{MM}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>MM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\textnormal{MM}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for a sufficiently large </span><math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Apart from adding a large regularization, is there any other way to prevent GRW from obtaining almost the same model as ERM in classification?
The main reason why GRW always converges to the max-margin classifier regardless of the weights <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is that the logistic loss is exponentially tailed.
Thus, one way to ensure that the sample weights have an impact is to use a polynomially tailed loss.
For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib150" title="" class="ltx_ref">150</a>]</cite> defined the following polynomially tailed loss for linear classifiers:</p>
<table id="S25.Ex90" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S25.Ex90.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\ell_{\alpha,\beta}(\hat{y},y)=\left\{\begin{aligned} \ell_{\textrm{left}}(\hat{y},y),\quad&amp;\text{if }\hat{y}y&lt;\beta;\\
\frac{1}{{\left[\hat{y}y-(\beta-1)\right]}^{\alpha}},\quad&amp;\text{if }\hat{y}y\geq\beta,\end{aligned}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>left</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\alpha,\beta}(\hat{y},y)=\left\{\begin{aligned} \ell_{\textrm{left}}(\hat{y},y),\quad&amp;\text{if }\hat{y}y&lt;\beta;\\
\frac{1}{{\left[\hat{y}y-(\beta-1)\right]}^{\alpha}},\quad&amp;\text{if }\hat{y}y\geq\beta,\end{aligned}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\textrm{left}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>left</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\textrm{left}}</annotation></semantics></math> is any function such that the overall loss function <math id="S25.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\alpha,\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\alpha,\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, differentiable and strictly decreasing.
Here we empirically compare between the logistic loss and this polynomially tailed loss on the six MNIST images we used earlier.
The results are plotted in <a href="#S25.F21" title="In Wide neural networks, classification tasks. ‣ 25 Generalized Reweighting (GRW) Versus ERM ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>, and we can observe the following:</p>
<ul id="S25.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S25.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S25.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For either loss function, the training loss of each method converges to 0.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S25.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S25.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S25.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In theory the norm of the ERM model will explode to infinity, but in reality it will not because once the training loss becomes extremely small, it will turn into zero in the floating number representation, and thus the training halts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S25.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S25.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S25.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can see a fundamental difference between the logistic loss and the polynomially tailed loss. For the logistic loss, the norm of the gap between importance weighting (or Group DRO) and ERM will converge to around 0.06 when the training stops, while for the polynomially tailed loss, the norm will be larger than 0.22 and will keep growing, which shows that for the polynomially tailed loss the normalized model weights do not converge to the same point.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S25.F21" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S25.F21.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="347.66" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 446.41 347.66" width="446.41"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,347.66) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(36.41,0) translate(0,249.32)"><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -36.41 -26.84)"><g class="ltx_nestedsvg" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 0) translate(36.41,0) translate(0,26.85)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 2.5 0 L 2.5 5.91 M 35.79 0 L 35.79 5.91 M 69.08 0 L 69.08 5.91 M 2.5 57.07 L 2.5 51.17 M 35.79 57.07 L 35.79 51.17 M 69.08 57.07 L 69.08 51.17"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" stroke="#808080" stroke-width="0.2pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 5.91 0 M 0 14.27 L 5.91 14.27 M 0 28.54 L 5.91 28.54 M 0 42.81 L 5.91 42.81 M 0 57.07 L 5.91 57.07 M 85.72 0 L 79.81 0 M 85.72 14.27 L 79.81 14.27 M 85.72 28.54 L 79.81 28.54 M 85.72 42.81 L 79.81 42.81 M 85.72 57.07 L 79.81 57.07"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 57.07 L 85.72 57.07 L 85.72 0 L 0 0 Z"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -0.96 -13.81)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S25.F21.pic1.m1" class="ltx_