height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="42.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S27.F24.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">DORO</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 123.49 17.12 L 106.83 17.12"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.64pt" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 106.83 17.12)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.16 2.88 C -1.98 1.8 0 0.18 0.54 0 C 0 -0.18 -1.98 -1.8 -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 158.78 17.12 L 175.44 17.12"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.64pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 175.44 17.12)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.16 2.88 C -1.98 1.8 0 0.18 0.54 0 C 0 -0.18 -1.98 -1.8 -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 91.74 34.82 L 82.14 34.82"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.64pt" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 82.14 34.82)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.16 2.88 C -1.98 1.8 0 0.18 0.54 0 C 0 -0.18 -1.98 -1.8 -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 130.55 34.82 L 140.15 34.82"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.64pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 140.15 34.82)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.16 2.88 C -1.98 1.8 0 0.18 0.54 0 C 0 -0.18 -1.98 -1.8 -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 5.29 L 105.85 5.29"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -1.17 5.24)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="19.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S27.F24.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 74.97 -32.76)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.91em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="26.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S27.F24.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">Loss</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 -37.07 -30.11)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.56em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="118.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S27.F24.pic1.4" class="ltx_text">Cumulative density</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 24: </span>Comparison between DRO and DORO for CVaR.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S27.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a DRO method is defined by the constraint <math id="S27.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(Q\parallel P)\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(Q\parallel P)\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, where the divergence function <math id="S27.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(Q\parallel P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(Q\parallel P)</annotation></semantics></math> measures the difference between <math id="S27.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S27.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
There are two large families of divergence functions, namely integral probability metrics (IPMs) and <math id="S27.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences.
An IPM is defined as <math id="S27.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(Q\parallel P)=\sup_{f\in{\mathcal{F}}}{\left[\mathbb{E}_{X\sim Q}f(X)-\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim P}f(Y)\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(Q\parallel P)=\sup_{f\in{\mathcal{F}}}{\left[\mathbb{E}_{X\sim Q}f(X)-\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim P}f(Y)\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> for some function class <math id="S27.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is symmetric, so it is also written as <math id="S27.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(Q,P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(Q,P)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Examples include the total variation (TV) distance defined as <math id="S27.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(Q,P)=\frac{1}{2}\int|Q(x)-P(x)|dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(Q,P)=\frac{1}{2}\int|Q(x)-P(x)|dx</annotation></semantics></math>,
maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) defined as <math id="S27.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(Q,P)={\left\|\mathbb{E}_{X\sim Q}\pi(X)-\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim P}\pi(Y)\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(Q,P)={\left\|\mathbb{E}_{X\sim Q}\pi(X)-\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim P}\pi(Y)\right\|}_{{\mathcal{H}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some feature map <math id="S27.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and some RKHS <math id="S27.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the Wasserstein distance defined as <math id="S27.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(Q,P)=\inf_{\gamma\in\Gamma(Q,P)}\int\rho(x,y)d\gamma(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(Q,P)=\inf_{\gamma\in\Gamma(Q,P)}\int\rho(x,y)d\gamma(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> for some metric function <math id="S27.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S27.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S27.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence is defined as <math id="S27.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{f}(Q\parallel P)=\int f{\left(\frac{dQ}{dP}\right)}dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{f}(Q\parallel P)=\int f{\left(\frac{dQ}{dP}\right)}dP</annotation></semantics></math> for some function <math id="S27.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. An <math id="S27.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence is not necessarily symmetric.
For example, when <math id="S27.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)=-\log t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)=-\log t</annotation></semantics></math>, then the <math id="S27.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence becomes the popular KL-divergence <math id="S27.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{f}(Q\parallel P)=D_{KL}(P\parallel Q)=\int\log{\left(\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\right)}dP(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{f}(Q\parallel P)=D_{KL}(P\parallel Q)=\int\log{\left(\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}\right)}dP(x)</annotation></semantics></math> (note that <math id="S27.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S27.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> are reversed).
The TV distance is the only non-trivial divergence function that is both an IPM and an <math id="S27.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S27.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The Cressie-Read family of Rényi divergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> is a family of <math id="S27.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences.
For any <math id="S27.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the divergence is defined as</p>
<table id="S27.Ex91" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.Ex91.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\beta}(Q\parallel P)=\int f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{dQ}{dP}\right)}dP,\qquad\text{where }\;f_{\beta}(t)=\frac{1}{\beta(\beta-1)}{\left(t^{\beta}-\beta t+\beta-1\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>where </mtext><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>β</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\beta}(Q\parallel P)=\int f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{dQ}{dP}\right)}dP,\qquad\text{where }\;f_{\beta}(t)=\frac{1}{\beta(\beta-1)}{\left(t^{\beta}-\beta t+\beta-1\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S27.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The reason why we are interested in this family is that the DRO risk <a href="#S24.E26" title="In 24 Reweighting and DRO ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> <span id="S27.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w.r.t.</span> <math id="S27.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> has the following dual characterization (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, Lemma 1]</cite>):</p>
<table id="S27.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\beta},\rho}(f;P)=\inf_{\eta\in\mathbb{R}}{\left\{c_{\beta}(\rho)\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}{\left[{\left(\ell{\left(f(X),Y\right)}-\eta\right)}_{+}^{\beta_{*}}\right]}^{\frac{1}{\beta_{*}}}+\eta\right\}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\beta},\rho}(f;P)=\inf_{\eta\in\mathbb{R}}{\left\{c_{\beta}(\rho)\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}{\left[{\left(\ell{\left(f(X),Y\right)}-\eta\right)}_{+}^{\beta_{*}}\right]}^{\frac{1}{\beta_{*}}}+\eta\right\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S27.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S27.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)_{+}=\max{\left\{x,0\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)_{+}=\max{\left\{x,0\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S27.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{*}=\frac{\beta}{\beta-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{*}=\frac{\beta}{\beta-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S27.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\beta}(\rho)={\left(1+\beta(\beta-1)\rho\right)}^{1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\beta}(\rho)={\left(1+\beta(\beta-1)\rho\right)}^{1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S27.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We first show the relationship between the DRO loss and the worst-group risk.
Suppose there are <math id="S27.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> groups <math id="S27.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}_{1},\cdots,{\mathcal{D}}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}_{1},\cdots,{\mathcal{D}}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S27.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{k}(x,y)=P((x,y)|(x,y)\in{\mathcal{D}}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{k}(x,y)=P((x,y)|(x,y)\in{\mathcal{D}}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the worst-group risk is given by</p>
<table id="S27.Ex92" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.Ex92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f;P)=\max_{k=1,\cdots,K}{\mathcal{R}}(f;P_{k})=\max_{k=1,\cdots,K}\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}[\ell(f(X),Y)|(X,Y)\in{\mathcal{D}}_{k}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f;P)=\max_{k=1,\cdots,K}{\mathcal{R}}(f;P_{k})=\max_{k=1,\cdots,K}\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}[\ell(f(X),Y)|(X,Y)\in{\mathcal{D}}_{k}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S27.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the scenario where we do not know <math id="S27.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}_{1},\cdots,{\mathcal{D}}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}_{1},\cdots,{\mathcal{D}}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> at train time, but only know that the smallest group is at least <math id="S27.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> of the population size.
Then, we can use the DRO risk given by the Cressie-Read family to obtain a surrogate of the worst-group risk, as shown by the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.12</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\min_{k=1,\cdots,K}P({\mathcal{D}}_{k})\leq\exp(-1)\approx 36.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>36.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\min_{k=1,\cdots,K}P({\mathcal{D}}_{k})\leq\exp(-1)\approx 36.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> be the minima group size, and define <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=f_{\beta}(\frac{1}{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=f_{\beta}(\frac{1}{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f;P)\leq{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\beta},\rho}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f;P)\leq{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\beta},\rho}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S27.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S27.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
Note that <math id="S27.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\beta}^{\prime}(t)=\frac{1}{\beta-1}{\left(t^{\beta-1}-1\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\beta}^{\prime}(t)=\frac{1}{\beta-1}{\left(t^{\beta-1}-1\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S27.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\beta}^{\prime}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\beta}^{\prime}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is decreasing when <math id="S27.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and increasing when <math id="S27.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\left[1,\frac{1}{\alpha}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\left[1,\frac{1}{\alpha}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that <math id="S27.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\beta}(t)\leq\max{\left\{f_{\beta}(0),f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\beta}(t)\leq\max{\left\{f_{\beta}(0),f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We can further verify that <math id="S27.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}-f_{\beta}(0)=\frac{1}{\beta(\beta-1)}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha^{\beta}}-\frac{\beta}{\alpha}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}-f_{\beta}(0)=\frac{1}{\beta(\beta-1)}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha^{\beta}}-\frac{\beta}{\alpha}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is non-negative when <math id="S27.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\leq\beta^{-\frac{1}{\beta-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\leq\beta^{-\frac{1}{\beta-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S27.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S27.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-\frac{1}{\beta-1}}\geq\exp(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-\frac{1}{\beta-1}}\geq\exp(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we have essentially proved that when <math id="S27.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\leq\exp(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\leq\exp(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is</p>
<table id="S27.Ex93" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.Ex93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall t\in{\left[0,\frac{1}{\alpha}\right]},\;f_{\beta}(t)\leq f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall t\in{\left[0,\frac{1}{\alpha}\right]},\;f_{\beta}(t)\leq f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S27.p8.2" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S27.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, there is <math id="S27.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{dP_{k}}{dP}\leq\frac{1}{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{dP_{k}}{dP}\leq\frac{1}{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we have</p>
<table id="S27.Ex94" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.Ex94.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\beta}(P_{k}\parallel P)=\int f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{dP_{k}}{dP}\right)}dP\leq\int f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}dP=f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\beta}(P_{k}\parallel P)=\int f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{dP_{k}}{dP}\right)}dP\leq\int f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)}dP=f_{\beta}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}\right)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S27.p8.3" class="ltx_p">which combined with the definition of <math id="S27.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\beta},\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\beta},\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> completes the proof.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S27.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p9.1" class="ltx_p">While the Cressie-Read family only defines the <math id="S27.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence for finite <math id="S27.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in(1,+\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in(1,+\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be shown that the dual characterization <a href="#S27.E31" title="In 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a> is valid for <math id="S27.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as well, for which <math id="S27.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes CVaR.
Our analysis below focuses on two cases:
(i) <math id="S27.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to CVaR;
(ii) <math id="S27.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to the <math id="S27.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-DRO risk used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>.
<a href="#S27.T10" title="In 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> summarizes the relevant information about these two DRO risks.
We will denote the CVaR risk by <math id="S27.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S27.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-DRO risk by <math id="S27.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\chi^{2},\rho}}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\chi^{2},\rho}}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S27.T10" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S27.T10.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S27.T10.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S27.T10.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S27.T10.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S27.T10.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S27.T10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S27.T10.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S27.T10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{*}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S27.T10.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S27.T10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S27.T10.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S27.T10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\beta}(\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\beta}(\rho)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S27.T10.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S27.T10.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\beta}(Q\parallel P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\beta}(Q\parallel P)</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S27.T10.2.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Risk notation</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S27.T10.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S27.T10.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S27.T10.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CVaR</span></th>
<td id="S27.T10.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S27.T10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S27.T10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S27.T10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S27.T10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S27.T10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup\log\frac{dQ}{dP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup\log\frac{dQ}{dP}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S27.T10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S27.T10.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S27.T10.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><math id="S27.T10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝌</mi><mn>𝟐</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S27.T10.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DRO</span></th>
<td id="S27.T10.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S27.T10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S27.T10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S27.T10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S27.T10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{1+{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{1+{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S27.T10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\int{\left(\frac{dQ}{dP}-1\right)}^{2}dP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\int{\left(\frac{dQ}{dP}-1\right)}^{2}dP</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S27.T10.2.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S27.T10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\chi^{2},\rho}}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\chi^{2},\rho}}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>The two DRO risks studied in our analysis.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S27.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p10.1" class="ltx_p">From <a href="#S27.E31" title="In 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>, we derive the following dual form of CVaR:</p>
<table id="S27.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)=\inf_{\eta\in\mathbb{R}}{\left\{\alpha^{-1}\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}{\left[{\left(\ell{\left(f(X),Y\right)}-\eta\right)}_{+}\right]}+\eta\right\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)=\inf_{\eta\in\mathbb{R}}{\left\{\alpha^{-1}\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}{\left[{\left(\ell{\left(f(X),Y\right)}-\eta\right)}_{+}\right]}+\eta\right\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S27.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p11.1" class="ltx_p">It is easy to see that the optimal <math id="S27.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S27.E32" title="In 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a> is the <math id="S27.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-quantile of the loss defined as</p>
<table id="S27.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.E33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{f;P}(\alpha)=\inf_{q}{\left\{P_{(X,Y)\sim P}{\left[\ell{\left(f(X),Y\right)}&gt;q\right]}\leq\alpha\right\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mi>q</mi></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{f;P}(\alpha)=\inf_{q}{\left\{P_{(X,Y)\sim P}{\left[\ell{\left(f(X),Y\right)}&gt;q\right]}\leq\alpha\right\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S27.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem12" title="Proposition 6.12. ‣ 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a> implies that both CVaR and <math id="S27.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-risks are upper bounds of <math id="S27.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.13</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\min_{k=1,\cdots,K}P({\mathcal{D}}_{k})\leq\exp(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\min_{k=1,\cdots,K}P({\mathcal{D}}_{k})\leq\exp(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimal group size, and <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{1}{2}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{1}{2}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f;P)\leq\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)\leq{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\chi^{2}},\rho}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f;P)\leq\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)\leq{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\chi^{2}},\rho}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S27.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S27.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
Denote <math id="S27.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}=P({\mathcal{D}}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}=P({\mathcal{D}}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S27.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S27.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x,y)=p_{k}P(x,y|{\mathcal{D}}_{k})+(1-p_{k})P(x,y|\overline{{\mathcal{D}}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x,y)=p_{k}P(x,y|{\mathcal{D}}_{k})+(1-p_{k})P(x,y|\overline{{\mathcal{D}}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S27.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=P_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=P_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S27.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}(x,y)=\frac{p_{k}-\alpha}{1-\alpha}P(x,y|{\mathcal{D}}_{k})+\frac{1-p_{k}}{1-\alpha}P(x,y|\overline{{\mathcal{D}}_{k}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}(x,y)=\frac{p_{k}-\alpha}{1-\alpha}P(x,y|{\mathcal{D}}_{k})+\frac{1-p_{k}}{1-\alpha}P(x,y|\overline{{\mathcal{D}}_{k}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, <math id="S27.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\alpha Q+(1-\alpha)Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\alpha Q+(1-\alpha)Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which by the definition of CVaR implies that <math id="S27.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{P_{k}}[\ell(f(X),Y)]\leq\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{P_{k}}[\ell(f(X),Y)]\leq\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S27.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f;P)\leq\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f;P)\leq\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand, for any <math id="S27.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> such that there exists <math id="S27.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S27.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\alpha Q+(1-\alpha)Q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\alpha Q+(1-\alpha)Q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is <math id="S27.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{dQ}{dP}(x,y)\leq\frac{1}{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{dQ}{dP}(x,y)\leq\frac{1}{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S27.p13.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a.e.</span>.
Thus, <math id="S27.p13.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\chi^{2}}(Q\parallel P)\leq\frac{1}{2}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}=\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\chi^{2}}(Q\parallel P)\leq\frac{1}{2}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}=\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that <math id="S27.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)\leq{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\chi^{2}},\rho}(f;P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha}(f;P)\leq{\mathcal{R}}_{D_{\chi^{2}},\rho}(f;P)</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<section id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Method.</h4>

<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model the outliers in the dataset with <span id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Huber’s <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-contamination model</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>.
Let <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be the clean distribution without outliers, and <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\rm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\rm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the observed contaminated training distribution.
Then, this model assumes that</p>
<table id="S27.Ex95" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.Ex95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\rm{train}}=(1-\epsilon)P+\epsilon\tilde{P},\quad\text{where }\tilde{P}\text{ is an arbitrary distribution, and }0&lt;\epsilon&lt;\frac{1}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is an arbitrary distribution, and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\rm{train}}=(1-\epsilon)P+\epsilon\tilde{P},\quad\text{where }\tilde{P}\text{ is an arbitrary distribution, and }0&lt;\epsilon&lt;\frac{1}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As we saw in <a href="#S27.F24" title="In 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>, DORO ignores the worst samples where the loss is the highest.
With this insight, we define the DORO risk as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.14</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">expected <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-DORO risk</span> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S27.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho,\epsilon}(f;P_{\rm{train}})=\inf_{P^{\prime}}{\left\{{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho(f;P^{\prime})}\;\middle|\;\exists\;\tilde{P}^{\prime}\;\text{s.t.}\;P_{\rm{train}}=(1-\epsilon)P^{\prime}+\epsilon\tilde{P}^{\prime}\right\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">∃</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">s.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho,\epsilon}(f;P_{\rm{train}})=\inf_{P^{\prime}}{\left\{{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho(f;P^{\prime})}\;\middle|\;\exists\;\tilde{P}^{\prime}\;\text{s.t.}\;P_{\rm{train}}=(1-\epsilon)P^{\prime}+\epsilon\tilde{P}^{\prime}\right\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The DORO risk has the following relationship with the total variation.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.15</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{TV}(P,Q)=\frac{1}{2}\int_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}}|P(z)-Q(z)|dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>TV</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{TV}(P,Q)=\frac{1}{2}\int_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}}|P(z)-Q(z)|dz</annotation></semantics></math> be the total variation, and <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\rm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\rm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> be given by the Huber’s model. Then the DORO risk can be lower bounded by</span></p>
<table id="S27.Ex96" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.Ex96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho,\epsilon}(\theta;P_{\rm{train}})\geq\inf_{P^{\prime\prime}}{\left\{{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho}(\theta;P^{\prime\prime}):\textnormal{TV}(P,P^{\prime\prime})\leq\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}\right\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>TV</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho,\epsilon}(\theta;P_{\rm{train}})\geq\inf_{P^{\prime\prime}}{\left\{{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho}(\theta;P^{\prime\prime}):\textnormal{TV}(P,P^{\prime\prime})\leq\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}\right\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof </span>
For any <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\rm{train}}=(1-\epsilon)P^{\prime}+\epsilon\tilde{P^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\rm{train}}=(1-\epsilon)P^{\prime}+\epsilon\tilde{P^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(z)=\min{\left\{P(z),P^{\prime}(z)\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(z)=\min{\left\{P(z),P^{\prime}(z)\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we have</p>
<table id="S27.Ex97" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.Ex97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\epsilon)U(z)+\epsilon\tilde{P}(z)+\epsilon\tilde{P^{\prime}}(z)\geq P_{\rm{train}}(z)\quad\text{for any }z\in{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for any </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\epsilon)U(z)+\epsilon\tilde{P}(z)+\epsilon\tilde{P^{\prime}}(z)\geq P_{\rm{train}}(z)\quad\text{for any }z\in{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{Y}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">as both <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{P^{\prime}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{P^{\prime}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> are non-negative. Integrating both sides produces <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}}U(z)dz\geq\frac{1-2\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{{\mathcal{X}}\times{\mathcal{Y}}}U(z)dz\geq\frac{1-2\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{TV}(P,P^{\prime})\leq\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>TV</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{TV}(P,P^{\prime})\leq\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we have</p>
<table id="S27.Ex98" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S27.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho}(\theta;P^{\prime})\geq\inf_{P^{\prime\prime}}{\left\{{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho}(\theta,P^{\prime\prime}):\textnormal{TV}(P,P^{\prime\prime})\leq\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}\right\}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>TV</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho}(\theta;P^{\prime})\geq\inf_{P^{\prime\prime}}{\left\{{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho}(\theta,P^{\prime\prime}):\textnormal{TV}(P,P^{\prime\prime})\leq\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}\right\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">which combined with the definition of the DORO risk proves the result.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:6.5pt;height:6.5pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.7pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With the Cressie-Read family, the DORO risk has the following dual formula:</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.16</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter12.A8" title="Appendix 12.H Proof of Proposition ‣ Chapter 12 Proofs for Chapter 6 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.H</span></a>)<span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> be a continuous non-negative loss function, and suppose <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\rm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\rm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous distribution.
Then, we have</span></p>
<table id="S27.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S27.Ex99"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S27.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho,\epsilon}(f;P_{\rm{train}})=\inf_{\eta}{\left\{c_{\beta}(\rho)\underset{(X,Y)\sim P_{\rm{train}}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left(\ell-\eta\right)}_{+}^{\beta_{*}}\;\middle|\;\Pr_{(X^{\prime},Y^{\prime})\sim P_{\rm{train}}}{\left\{\ell&gt;\ell^{\prime}\right\}}\geq\epsilon\right]}^{\frac{1}{\beta_{*}}}+\eta\right\}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mi>η</mi></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">train</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.447em" stretchy="true">|</mo><munder><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mfrac></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{R}}_{D,\rho,\epsilon}(f;P_{\rm{train}})=\inf_{\eta}{\left\{c_{\beta}(\rho)\underset{(X,Y)\sim P_{\rm{train}}}{\mathbb{E}}{\left[{\left(\ell-\eta\right)}_{+}^{\beta_{*}}\;\middle|\;\Pr_{(X^{\prime},Y^{\prime})\sim P_{\rm{train}}}{\left\{\ell&gt;\ell^{\prime}\right\}}\geq\epsilon\right]}^{\frac{1}{\beta_{*}}}+\eta\right\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=\ell(f(X),Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=\ell(f(X),Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}=\ell(f(X^{\prime}),Y^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}=\ell(f(X^{\prime}),Y^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">With this dual formula, the DORO risk can be minimized using <a href="#alg5" title="In Method. ‣ 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
For each batch of samples, this algorithm first sorts the samples by their training losses, and then finds the optimal <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in the above dual form.
For example, we can use Brent’s method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> to find <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, this <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is updated to minimize the dual form.
This algorithm is inspired by the ITLM algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib127" title="" class="ltx_ref">127</a>]</cite>, in which an alternative approach to making DRO more robust to outliers was proposed—removing the potential outliers from the dataset via data preprocessing.
In comparison, DORO does not throw away any data.
In addition, preprocessing methods cannot cope with online data where new samples are received in a stream, but DORO is still feasible.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg5" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 5</span> </span> Minimizing the DORO risk with <math id="alg5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> Divergence</figcaption>
<div id="alg5.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg5.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
              <span id="alg5.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Batch size <math id="alg5.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, outlier fraction <math id="alg5.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, minimal group size <math id="alg5.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, initial model weight <math id="alg5.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
              <span id="alg5.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each iteration <span id="alg5.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               Sample a batch <math id="alg5.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},y_{1}),\cdots,(x_{n},y_{n})\sim P_{\rm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},y_{1}),\cdots,(x_{n},y_{n})\sim P_{\rm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               Compute the sample losses: <math id="alg5.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}=\ell(f_{\theta}(x_{i}),y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}=\ell(f_{\theta}(x_{i}),y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="alg5.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\cdots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\cdots,n</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               Sort the sample losses: <math id="alg5.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i_{1}}\geq\cdots\geq\ell_{i_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i_{1}}\geq\cdots\geq\ell_{i_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               Define <math id="alg5.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta,\eta)=c_{\beta}(\rho)\cdot[\frac{1}{n-\lfloor\epsilon n\rfloor}\sum_{j=\lfloor\epsilon n\rfloor+1}^{n}(\ell(f_{\theta}(x_{i_{j}}),y_{i_{j}})-\eta)_{+}^{\beta_{*}}]^{\frac{1}{\beta_{*}}}+\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta,\eta)=c_{\beta}(\rho)\cdot[\frac{1}{n-\lfloor\epsilon n\rfloor}\sum_{j=\lfloor\epsilon n\rfloor+1}^{n}(\ell(f_{\theta}(x_{i_{j}}),y_{i_{j}})-\eta)_{+}^{\beta_{*}}]^{\frac{1}{\beta_{*}}}+\eta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               Find <math id="alg5.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{*}=\argmin_{\eta}F(\theta,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>η</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{*}=\argmin_{\eta}F(\theta,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg5.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                
                
               Update <math id="alg5.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> by one step to minimize <math id="alg5.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\theta)=F(\theta,\eta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\theta)=F(\theta,\eta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> with some gradient method

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theoretical guarantee.</h4>

<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that the DORO risk is a surrogate of the worst-group risk, meaning that the DORO risk is an upper bound.
This result parallels <a href="#chapter6.Thmtheorem13" title="Corollary 6.13. ‣ 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.13</span></a> in the uncontaminated setting, and guarantees that minimizing the DORO risk over <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\rm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\rm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> efficiently minimizes <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.17</span></span><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proof in <a href="#chapter12.A9" title="Appendix 12.I Proof of Theorem ‣ Chapter 12 Proofs for Chapter 6 ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.I</span></a>)<span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\min_{k=1,\cdots,K}P({\mathcal{D}}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\min_{k=1,\cdots,K}P({\mathcal{D}}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{1}{2}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{1}{2}{\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}-1\right)}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\rm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\rm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the Huber’s <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-contamination model.
Suppose <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-negative loss function with a uniformly bounded second moment: <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}{\left[\ell(f_{\theta}(X),Y)^{2}\right]}\leq\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}{\left[\ell(f_{\theta}(X),Y)^{2}\right]}\leq\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="chapter6.Thmtheorem17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have</span></p>
<table id="S27.E35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S27.E35X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S27.E35X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f_{\theta};P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{R}}_{\max}(f_{\theta};P)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S27.E35X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\max{\left\{3\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha,\epsilon}(f_{\theta};P_{\rm{train}}),3\alpha^{-1}\sigma\sqrt{\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}}\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>CVaR</mtext><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\max{\left\{3\textnormal{CVaR}_{\alpha,\epsilon}(f_{\theta};P_{\rm{train}}),3\alpha^{-1}\sigma\sqrt{\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}}\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S27.E35Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S27.E35Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\max{\left\{3D_{\chi^{2},\rho,\epsilon}(f_{\theta};P_{\rm{train}}),3\alpha^{-1}\sigma\sqrt{\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}}\right\}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\max{\left\{3D_{\chi^{2},\rho,\epsilon}(f_{\theta};P_{\rm{train}}),3\alpha^{-1}\sigma\sqrt{\frac{\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}}\right\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Empirical evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test DORO on three datasets: COMPAS, CelebA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>]</cite> and CivilComments-Wilds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite>.
CelebA is a facial recognition dataset, where the target is whether the person has blond hair.
CivilComments-Wilds is a toxicity identification NLP dataset, where the target is whether an online post contains toxic contents.
All targets are binary.
For COMPAS, we randomly sample 70% of the samples to be the training data.
The other two datasetse have official train-val-test splits.
On COMPAS we define 4 groups, and on each of the other datasets we define 16 groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use a two-layer ReLU-activated feed-forward neural network on the COMPAS dataset, a ResNet18 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> on CelebA, and a BERT-base-uncased <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> on CivilComments-Wilds.
Each algorithm is run 300 epochs on COMPAS, 30 epochs on CelebA and 5 epochs on CivilComments-Wilds.
The best model is selected based on the worst-group accuracy on the validation set.
Note that in reality, the worst-group accuracy is not available because we might now know the group membership of each sample.
Therefore, this model selection strategy is an oracle one.
We will discuss more on this point later.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S27.T11" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S27.T11.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S27.T11.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S27.T11.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S27.T11.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span></th>
<th id="S27.T11.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S27.T11.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S27.T11.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S27.T11.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average Accuracy</span></th>
<th id="S27.T11.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S27.T11.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Worst-group Accuracy</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
