<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 11: </span>Average/worst-group test accuracies of the selected models. (%)</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S27.T11" title="In Empirical evaluation. ‣ 27 Distributionally and Outlier Robust Optimization ‣ Chapter 6 Generalization Under Distribution Shift ‣ Contextures: The Mechanism of Representation Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> reports the 95% confidence intervals of the mean test accuracies on each dataset.
For DRO and DORO, we do a grid search to pick the best <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S27.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> that achieve the best worst-group accuracy.
Each experiment is repeated 10 times on COMPAS and CelebA, and 5 times on CivilComments-Wilds with different random seeds.
From the table, we can conclude that DORO consistently outperforms DRO in terms of the average and worst-group test accuracies.</p>
</div>
